<div class="bt">
  <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
  <div class="vol">Orach Chaim — Volume 3</div>
</div>

<div class="hh">
  <h2>Hilchos Techumin</h2>
  <span class="hd"></span>
</div>

<div class="hs">
Continuation of Halachah 7 — Based on Likutay Moharan I, Torah 6:<br>
<em>"Kra es Yehoshua v'atzavenu"</em>
</div>

<div class="sl">
Sections 19–74 · Continuing from Hilchos Shabbos 7, sections 1–18
</div>


<!-- ══════════ Section 19 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 19 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">Shabbos boundary — <em>techum Shabbos</em></span> — of two thousand <em>amos</em>, which is the aspect of <span class="kt">two <em>Alephs</em></span>. For Shabbos is the aspect of teshuvah, through which is formed the aspect of the <em>Aleph</em> mentioned above, which is the aspect of <em>Adam</em> etc. And all this is accomplished through the aspect of <em>Ehyeh</em> — the aspect of <span class="sq"><em>ana zamin l'mihavei</em></span> [I am destined to become] — and there is no <em>Ehyeh</em> without an <em>Aleph</em>, the aspect of the <em>Aleph</em> mentioned above. And one must do <span class="kt">teshuvah upon teshuvah</span>, and one must also be <span class="kt">expert in halachah</span> — expert in <em>ratzo</em>, expert in <em>shov</em>, expert in entering, expert in exiting — in the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are; and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You are there"</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="kt">secret of the <em>kavanos</em> of Elul</span>, through which one merits to walk in the ways of teshuvah. And this itself is the aspect of teshuvah upon teshuvah, as is brought in our words elsewhere.
</p>

<p class="p">
For it is impossible to merit the aforementioned expertise except for one who is <span class="kt">strong within himself to walk in the ways of teshuvah constantly</span>, however things may be etc. And all this is impossible to merit except through the power of the Tzadik, who does teshuvah upon teshuvah constantly etc., as mentioned above. And all this is the aspect of Shabbos, as mentioned above. Therefore, <em>techum Shabbos</em> is two thousand <em>amos</em> — the aspect of two <em>Alephs</em> mentioned above. For teshuvah is the aspect of <em>Aleph</em>, the aspect of <em>Ehyeh</em>, but one needs teshuvah upon teshuvah, and one also needs the two expertises mentioned above — the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven"</span> etc. <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> etc. Therefore they are two <em>Alephs</em>, for each teshuvah is the aspect of an <em>Aleph</em>, the aspect of <em>Ehyeh</em> — <span class="sq"><em>ana zamin l'mihavei</em></span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
For on Shabbos it is said <span class="vs">(Shemos 16)</span>: <span class="v">"Let every man remain in his place; let no man go out from his place"</span> — for it is a day of rest and repose in which there is no upheaval of travel. For all the journeys and upheavals of the six weekdays are for the sake of <em>berurim</em> — to refine the holy sparks from amidst the <em>kelipos</em> that were scattered to the four corners of the earth through the sin of Adam HaRishon and through the sins of all the generations. For this reason one must travel the roads to refine them. And this itself is the aspect of what Rabeinu z"l said <span class="vs">(in siman 40 and in Part II, siman 62)</span> — that all journeys are for the purpose of rectifying the damage to <em>emunah</em> that is damaged through sins. For <span class="v">"all Your commandments are <em>emunah</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 119)</span>, and according to the damage one inflicts through some transgression or neglect of a mitzvah, so is the <em>emunah</em> damaged, <em>chas v'shalom</em>. Therefore one must return in teshuvah to rectify the damage of the sin, which is the damage to the holy <em>emunah</em> — and for this come the upheavals of travel, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
And one who merits to travel or journey on the road as befits a Jewish person — who remembers Hashem always, in the aspect of <span class="v">"when you sit in your house and when you walk on the way"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 6)</span>, and especially on the road where one must be extra careful to remember Hashem, for <span class="sq">"all roads are presumed dangerous"</span> — and the essential danger is that through the upheaval of travel one may forget Hashem, from which stem all dangers even physically — and one who merits to travel on the road as befits a Jew, then the road is in the aspect of <span class="v">"The way of <em>emunah</em> I have chosen"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 119)</span>, the aspect of ways of teshuvah. But the <span class="kt">essential rectification of the road, the rectification of <em>emunah</em>, the rectification of the ways of teshuvah, is on Shabbos</span> — for then there is no <em>birur</em>; rather, all the <em>berurim</em> of all the weekdays return to their place on Shabbos and come to their resting place, in the aspect of <span class="sq">"repose of tranquility, quietude, and security"</span> etc. And therefore, on Shabbos it is forbidden even to take a large stride, for then is the essential rectification of teshuvah, which is the aspect of Shabbos, which is the aspect of <span class="sq">"they say to him: Wait!"</span> — for it is impossible to take a large stride. For the completeness of teshuvah, the aspect of Shabbos, is in the aspect of <span class="kt">deliberateness</span> — the aspect of the aforementioned "Wait!" And therefore it is forbidden on Shabbos to walk on distant roads, only two thousand <em>amos</em> for a stroll — which is the aspect of <em>techum Shabbos</em>. For all the walking of Shabbos is only in the aspect of ways of teshuvah that are completed on Shabbos and come to their rest. Therefore they are two thousand <em>amos</em> — the aspect of the two <em>Alephs</em> mentioned above, which are the aspect of the ways of teshuvah, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 20 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 20 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"L'chah dodi neitzei ha'sadeh, nalinah ba'k'farim, nashkimah la'kramim"</span> — <span class="v">"Come, my beloved, let us go out to the field; let us lodge in the villages; let us arise early to the vineyards"</span> <span class="vs">(Shir HaShirim 7)</span>. <span class="v">"Let us lodge in the villages [<em>k'farim</em>]"</span> — and our Sages of blessed memory expounded <span class="vs">(Eiruvin 21b)</span>: <span class="sq">"among the <em>kof'rim</em>"</span> — those who are the wicked, who have distanced themselves greatly from Hashem, for they too must be returned in teshuvah. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You are there"</span> — to subdue and nullify their evil, which is the aspect of cutting off the seed of Amalek — the aspect of subduing the utter evil of the blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em> and refining the good from them, to return them in teshuvah. <span class="v">"Let us arise early to the vineyards [<em>kramim</em>]"</span> — these are the aspect of the upright and righteous, who are called <em>kramim</em>, as it is written <span class="vs">(Hoshea 2)</span>: <span class="v">"And I shall give her her vineyards"</span> etc., from there — as our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Rashi; see Yalkut Hoshea). And likewise <span class="vs">(Iyov 24)</span>: <span class="v">"He does not turn to the way of the vineyards"</span> — as Rashi explains there. For even the righteous must be in a state of constant teshuvah, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are."</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
And all this is said in Shir HaShirim regarding the holiness of Shabbos, as is brought in the <em>Ksavim</em> [writings of the Arizal] — for then the essential road, to go out to the field, is only for the sake of the aspect of the ways of teshuvah that are completed then, in the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are; and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You are there"</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"let us lodge among the villages, let us arise early to the vineyards"</span> etc., as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="v">"There I will give you my love"</span> — for this is the essential completeness of the bond and the love between <em>Knesses Yisrael</em> and Hashem: when the ways of teshuvah are completed as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="v">"The <em>dudaim</em> have given forth fragrance"</span> — the two types of <em>dudaim</em>, the good and the bad, as Rashi explains there — that is, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 21 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 21 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And for this reason, <span class="kt">people travel to the Tzadikim for Shabbos</span>. For every Tzadik is in the aspect of Moshe, as is written elsewhere. And the essential secret of the holiness of Shabbos — Hashem made it known to Moshe, that he should go and inform Yisrael, in the aspect of <span class="sq">"go and inform them,"</span> as mentioned above. And so in every generation, one must come to the true Tzadik, who will inform him of the holiness of Shabbos, which is the aspect of teshuvah. For certainly the essential reason people travel to the true Tzadikim is in order to merit teshuvah from wherever one may be. For one who travels and comes to the Rav for some other agenda — to receive from him some rabbinical appointment or other position etc. — this is not included in the category of drawing close to the Tzadik at all, for he is traveling for his own honor. But the <span class="kt">essential drawing close to the Tzadik is when one's intention is for Hashem alone</span> — that the Tzadik should draw him close to Hashem and bring him back from the place where he has fallen etc. And therefore, most people travel for Shabbos, for the essential way of teshuvah — which is the aspect of expertise in halachah etc., the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are; and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You are there"</span> — is through the holiness of Shabbos, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 22 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 22 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And therefore, all the <span class="kt">preparation for the holiness of Rosh HaShanah</span>, the Ten Days of Teshuvah, and Yom Kippur, is through the Shabbasos that precede them. As our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="vs">(Orach Chaim 428)</span> — they instituted that before Rosh HaShanah we read: the <em>t'lasa d'fur'anusa</em> [three of punishment] on the three Shabbasos of <em>Bein HaMetzarim</em>, and afterwards the <em>shiv'ah d'nechemta</em> [seven of consolation] and the <em>tartei d'siyuvta</em> [two of teshuvah]. For the essential holiness of Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur is to merit teshuvah, for they are the Ten Days of Teshuvah. And the essential teshuvah is through Shabbos, as mentioned above. For the essential unification between Moshe and Yehoshua — who are the aspects of the sun and the moon, the upper point and the lower point — is through the holiness of Shabbos, as mentioned above. For the essential rectification is that the aspect of Moshe etc. should illuminate the aspect of Yehoshua etc. Through this, the aspect of the <em>Aleph</em> is completed and <em>Adam</em> is formed to sit upon the Throne — and this is principally accomplished on Shabbos, in the aspect of <span class="sq">"On the seventh day He was elevated and sat upon His Throne of glory"</span> etc.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 23 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 23 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
For when a person sins, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, then according to his sin, the upper point withdraws and becomes concealed and does not illuminate the lower point. This is the aspect of separation — which is the essential damage of all sins, <em>chas v'shalom</em>. And through this, the Beis HaMikdash was destroyed — for it is the aspect of the upper point that has withdrawn upward and does not properly illuminate the lower point. For it is explained there in the aforementioned Torah that the Beis HaMikdash is the aspect of the upper point. Therefore, when sins prevailed and the upper point withdrew upward, the Beis HaMikdash was destroyed — the established place from which the upper point illuminated the lower point. And then it is the aspect of the departure of the <em>Shechinah</em> — the aspect of <span class="sq">"ten journeys the <em>Shechinah</em> traveled"</span> etc., as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="vs">(Rosh HaShanah 31a)</span>. And then all our vitality comes specifically from the concealment — from the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> [closed utterance] that gives life to all places distant from holiness etc., as is explained in the Torah <span class="sq">"V'ayeh ha'seh l'olah"</span> <span class="vs">(Part II, siman 12)</span>. And then one must cry out <span class="v">"Eichah"</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> [Where?] (as is brought from this in our words, in Hilchos Yibum V'Chalitzah, Halachah 4).
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">three Shabbasos of <em>Bein HaMetzarim</em></span>, which begin from the seventeenth of Tamuz when the <em>Luchos</em> were broken on account of the Sin of the Golden Calf. For the breaking of the <em>Luchos</em> that Moshe broke when he saw the enormity of the destruction — that they had done such unseemly deeds, which is the making of the Calf, which is <em>avodah zarah</em>, which is <span class="v">"filth without a place"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 28)</span>, and also <span class="v">"and they arose to make merry"</span> etc. <span class="vs">(Shemos 32)</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory expounded <span class="vs">(Tanchuma Ki Sisa 20)</span> and is brought in the commentary of Rashi — and then Moshe broke the <em>Luchos</em> and Hashem concurred with him, as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="vs">(Shabbos 87)</span>. For in this — that he broke the <em>Luchos</em> — he wrought a <span class="kt">wondrous rectification</span>. That is, he saw that now it was impossible in any manner to receive any holiness in a revealed manner, but only in a hidden manner — through the aspect of seeking and searching, which is the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above, which is the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> that gives life to all distant places, even those called <span class="sq">"filthy places,"</span> as is written in the aforementioned Torah. And this is the aspect of the breaking of the <em>Luchos</em> — the letters flew upward, and the holiness ascended upward. These are the letters of the Torah, through which His glory is principally revealed in the Ten Utterances, which are the aspect of the Ten Commandments. And through this, he drew forth the aspect of the rectification of seeking <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above — to show that now all rectification comes specifically through the concealment and the withdrawal. One who wishes to have compassion on himself, when he sees the enormity of the destruction, must search and seek: <span class="v">"Where is the place of His glory?"</span> — for His glory has withdrawn and become exceedingly concealed etc. And for this reason they cry out <span class="v">"Eichah"</span> at <em>chatzos</em> and on Tisha B'Av — the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
And then, during <em>Bein HaMetzarim</em>, all the rectification is through the three Shabbasos of <em>Bein HaMetzarim</em>, on which we read the <em>t'lasa d'fur'anusa</em> to arouse Yisrael in teshuvah. And the essential teshuvah then is through the seeking of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above, which is the aspect of the cry of <span class="v">"Eichah,"</span> as mentioned above. And therefore they are <span class="kt">three Shabbasos</span>, corresponding to the three uppermost [<em>sefiros</em>], which are the aspect of the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> — the aspect of the upper point, which is the aspect of <em>Kisei d'miskas'ya</em> etc. — which is the aspect of the <em>nekudas segol</em> that comprises three points. For the three uppermost are considered as one and are all included in the aspect of <em>Keser</em>, the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> — for regarding them it is said <span class="vs">(Chagigah 13)</span>: <span class="sq">"Into what is too wondrous for you, do not inquire"</span> — as is brought on the verse <span class="vs">(Devarim 4)</span>: <span class="v">"And from one end of heaven to the other end of heaven you inquire"</span> — which are the aspect of the seven days of creation etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
And the main point is: <span class="kt">the essential rectification is when there is unification between the sun and the moon</span>, between Moshe and Yehoshua — which are the aspects of the upper point and the lower point — which is when the seed of Amalek is subdued, which is the aspect of the intensity of the blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em> etc. And then the upper point illuminates the lower point — Moshe illuminates Yehoshua through the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em>. And then the <em>Aleph</em> is completed and <em>Adam</em> is formed to sit upon the Throne etc. And all this will not be in completeness until the Beis HaMikdash is built, which is the aspect of the upper point etc. But in the time of the <em>churban</em>, which is the aspect of <em>Bein HaMetzarim</em>, then the rectification is only through the cries of <span class="v">"Eichah"</span> — the aspect of seeking <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above. But even this is impossible to merit except through the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh — for then the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> illuminates, and then one merits to ascend there in holiness. For then is the ascent of all the worlds to the <em>ma'amar sasum</em>. And with this power, one can draw the holiness of Shabbos into the six weekdays — to merit complete teshuvah, even those who have fallen very far, who are in the aspect of <em>v'nafik</em> — that they too can find Hashem through seeking and searching: <span class="v">"Where is the place of His glory?"</span> (As is explained in all this in Hilchos Shabbos, Halachah 5; see there well.) And see in our words regarding the seeking of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above — that the main point is that even when a person sees in himself that he has distanced himself very, very far through his evil and wretched deeds, until he has fallen, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, to the aspect of <span class="sq">"filthy places"</span> — even then, <span class="kt">he should never despair of himself in any way</span>. Rather, he should seek and search and inquire even from there: <span class="v">"Where is the place of His glory?"</span> And through this, the descent will become the ultimate ascent, as is explained in siman 12 mentioned above and in the places where we have discussed this; see there.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 24 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 24 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And after Tisha B'Av we read the <span class="kt"><em>shiv'ah d'nechemta</em></span> [seven of consolation]. For through the mourning of <em>Klal Yisrael</em> during the three weeks and on Tisha B'Av, through this is drawn the aspect of the building of the Beis HaMikdash. As our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="vs">(Ta'anis 30b)</span>: <span class="sq">"Whoever mourns over Yerushalayim merits and sees her joy"</span> — as is brought in the <em>sefarim</em> that at the end of Tisha B'Av, the sprouting of the horn of Mashiach occurs. And then Hashem consoles us with all manner of consolations, which are the <em>shiv'ah d'nechemta</em> that we read from Shabbos Nachamu until Rosh HaShanah, in which begins the sweetening of the <em>charon af</em> [fierce anger] of the Sin of the Golden Calf, as is written elsewhere. And therefore they are <span class="kt">seven consolations</span>, corresponding to the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em> that illuminates the lower point — which are in their totality seven — through drawing the light from the aspect of the sprouting of the horn of Mashiach. For then all the above is rectified — the upper point illuminates the lower point properly through the aspect of the <em>Vav</em>, which is the essential rectification, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
And all this we read before Rosh HaShanah and the Ten Days of Teshuvah. For the essential holiness of Rosh HaShanah, the Ten Days of Teshuvah, and Yom Kippur, is through these Shabbasos on which we read these <em>Haftaros</em> — for the essential rectification is through Shabbos, as mentioned above. And therefore, we afterwards read the <span class="kt"><em>tartei d'siyuvta</em></span> [two of teshuvah] — <span class="sq">"two"</span> specifically — the aspect of teshuvah upon teshuvah, which is the aspect of <span class="sq">"Were Yisrael to keep two Shabbasos"</span> specifically, as mentioned above <span class="vs">(in section 8)</span>.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 25 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 25 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And it is known that all the rectifications that Yisrael are engaged in on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur — all are completed on <span class="kt">Shemini Atzeres and Simchas Torah</span>, for then is the essential completion of the rectification and the unification. And then we finish the Torah and begin it anew from <em>Beraishis</em> etc., and conclude with Shabbos — which is Parshas <span class="v">"Vay'chulu"</span>. For this is the essential completion of all the rectifications we have been engaged in from Elul and Rosh HaShanah etc. until now — all in order to draw and illuminate within us the holiness of Shabbos, which is the aspect of teshuvah, so that from now on all Yisrael will merit to begin fulfilling the Torah anew and to return in teshuvah, each one from wherever he may be. So that there will be drawn upon us the aspect of expertise in halachah mentioned above — the aspect of expert in entering, expert in exiting — the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are; and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You are there"</span> etc. Through this, the <em>Aleph</em> is completed and <em>Adam</em> is formed to sit upon the Throne etc. All of this is drawn through the holiness of Shabbos — for then the aspect of Moshe illuminates Yehoshua.
</p>

<p class="p">
And in this they conclude the Torah: that Moshe died and departed, and <span class="v">"Yehoshua bin Nun was filled with the spirit of wisdom, for Moshe had laid his hands"</span> etc. And they conclude: <span class="v">"And for all the mighty hand and all the great terror that Moshe wrought before the eyes of all Yisrael"</span> — that he <span class="kt">broke the <em>Luchos</em></span> before their eyes. For this was his greatest act of might, more than all the wonders he performed — as is understood from this verse according to the exposition of our Sages of blessed memory mentioned above, who expounded this verse regarding his breaking the <em>Luchos</em>. For certainly it is a wondrous and awesome act of might and an exceedingly awesome novelty. For through this he drew the aspect of the rectification of seeking <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above — through which there is great hope even for all those who have fallen very far, the aspect of <span class="sq">"filthy places,"</span> which are the aspects of exceedingly wretched thoughts of immorality and heresy and crooked thoughts of the heart against Hashem and difficulties etc. — which are the aspect of <em>avodah zarah</em> etc. And nevertheless, even for them there is hope if they wish, through the seeking of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above, which was drawn through the breaking of the <em>Luchos</em>, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
And therefore, afterwards we begin <em>Beraishis</em> — which is the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> [closed utterance], the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> mentioned above — and conclude with Shabbos. For the essential rectification is through the holiness of Shabbos, as mentioned above. (See regarding this matter above in section 23, and in Hilchos Nefilas Apayim, Halachah 6, section 7.)
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 26 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 26 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And therefore, <span class="kt">Moshe departed on Shabbos</span>, at the time of <em>Minchah</em>, at <em>ra'ava d'ra'avin</em> [the will of all wills]. For he drew the aforementioned rectification to all those who have fallen and are distant, as mentioned above. For he departed in awesome holiness to the aspect of <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> — for <span class="v">"no man knows his burial place"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 34:6)</span> — for there is the ultimate knowing of not-knowing, which is the aspect of the holiness of Shabbos (as is written in Likutay Tinyana, siman 83). And from there he draws illumination to the aspect of Yehoshua — so that Yehoshua can illuminate to all Yisrael this path: to strengthen and arouse and support all those who have fallen greatly, that they should never despair in any way. Rather, they too should search and seek His glory, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Where is the place of His glory?"</span> — until they merit each time to find Him, that He will shine His countenance upon them and they will be able to pray etc., they too.
</p>

<p class="p">
As is known — all this — to one who has begun to enter these holy paths: that even though it seems to him that he has already distanced himself so much that it is impossible for him to pray at all etc. — nevertheless, when he strengthens himself and asks and seeks <span class="v">"Ayeh"</span> etc., as mentioned above, in the meantime Hashem helps him so that he too can pray somewhat. And if he is strong in this all his days, his end will be good forever. And all this is the aspect of the expertise in halachah mentioned above, which is drawn through the true Tzadik — the aspect of Moshe — who makes known and draws the holiness of Shabbos, which is the aspect of teshuvah upon teshuvah always, however one may be, on every level — in ascent and in descent — until all Yisrael return to Hashem. Through this will come the complete redemption speedily in our days, amen selah.
</p>

<p class="p">
And therefore, on Simchas Torah we immediately read the <em>Haftarah</em> from Yehoshua <span class="vs">(Yehoshua 1)</span>: <span class="v">"Moshe My servant has died — now arise"</span> etc. (See below, section 39.)
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 27 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 27 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is <span class="vs">(Shemos 17)</span>: <span class="v">"For a hand is on the Throne of Y-ah — a war for Hashem with Amalek from generation to generation"</span> — that <span class="sq">"His Throne is not complete and His Name is not complete until the remembrance of Amalek is erased"</span> <span class="vs">(Tanchuma Ki Seitzei)</span>. For the main thing is to cut off the seed of Amalek, and this is accomplished through the aspect of Yehoshua specifically — in the aspect of <span class="v">"Go out and fight with Amalek"</span> — which is the aspect of the lower point. But it is impossible for Yehoshua to accomplish anything except through the power of Moshe, the aspect of the upper point, as it is written: <span class="v">"And it was, when Moshe raised his hand"</span> etc. And throughout the exile, Hashem fights with him, and then the <span class="kt">Throne is not complete</span> — the <em>Aleph</em> is lacking — and the <span class="kt">Name is not complete</span>. For when the aspect of <em>Adam</em> is formed to sit upon the Throne — this is the completeness of the Name <em>Havayah</em>, which is <em>Havayah</em> spelled with <em>Alephs</em>, equaling <em>Mah</em> [45] in <em>gematria</em> — equaling <em>Adam</em>. When the blood draws sustenance — when the <em>Aleph</em> in the middle of the <em>Vav</em> of the Name <em>Mah</em> withdraws, <em>chas v'shalom</em> — then it rises to <em>Dam</em> [blood], the aspect of the reverse of <em>Ehyeh</em> equaling <em>Dam</em>, as is explained in the writings of the Arizal in many places.
</p>

<p class="p">
And in every generation, to the degree that each person exerts himself to draw close to the true Tzadik amidst insults and much spilling of blood — so does the aspect of Moshe illuminate Yehoshua, the aspect of Rav and <em>talmid</em>, as mentioned above. Through this they cut off the seed of Amalek and all the rectifications are accomplished. But the war is very, very long. For Amalek has from of old been a <span class="sq">"strap of chastisement for Yisrael"</span> <span class="vs">(Tanchuma Chukas)</span> and spreads each time more and more, both collectively and individually, upon each person. Therefore the Torah warned us <span class="vs">(Devarim 25)</span>: <span class="v">"Remember what Amalek did to you"</span> — for one must remember well his great war, in order to flee each time to the true Tzadik and his holy Torah. For only he fights the war of Amalek in every generation — the aspect of one <span class="sq">"trained in war"</span> — until Mashiach comes speedily in our days, when his seed will be cut off completely, as it is written: <span class="v">"And it shall be, when Hashem your G-d gives you rest in the land… you shall erase the memory of Amalek"</span> etc.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 28 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 28 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">mitzvah of Milah</span>. Through the cutting of the <em>orlah</em> and the spilling of the infant's blood — this is the aspect of cutting off the seed of Amalek. For Amalek is the aspect of the contamination of the <em>orlah</em>, the damage to the <em>Bris</em>. Therefore he contested the mitzvah of <em>Milah</em> — he would cut off circumcisions and hurl them upward, as it is written: <span class="v">"And he cut off the tail — all the weakened ones behind you"</span> — as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Tanchuma Ki Seitzei)</span>. And the mitzvah of <em>pri'ah</em>, through which the <em>atarah</em> is revealed — this is the aspect of appointing a king. For the revelation of the <em>atarah</em> is the rectification of the <em>Bris</em>, through which one merits <em>malchus</em>, as with Yosef HaTzadik — as the Zohar says: <span class="sq">"One who guards the <em>Bris</em> merits kingship."</span> Afterwards the infant is given a name — the aspect of the upper point. For the name of a Jew is included in His Name — <span class="sq">"His Name is joined with our name"</span> — drawn from the root of <span class="sq">"Yisrael arose in thought first,"</span> which is the upper point. Then the <em>Aleph</em> is completed and <em>Adam</em> is formed. For only now is the infant in the aspect of <em>Adam</em> — when the <em>orlah</em> covers the <em>Bris</em>, this is not the form of <em>Adam</em>. Therefore only Yisrael who are circumcised are called <em>Adam</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore <span class="kt"><em>Milah</em> overrides Shabbos</span>. Though the holiness of Shabbos is exceedingly lofty — even the building of the Beis HaMikdash is pushed aside before it — nevertheless, it is impossible to draw the holiness of Shabbos into this world except through Yisrael specifically, as we say: <span class="sq">"You did not give it, Hashem our G-d, to the nations of the lands… for to Yisrael Your people You gave it in love."</span> For Yisrael and Shabbos are one aspect at their root, as understood from <em>Havdalah</em>: <span class="sq">"Who distinguishes between holy and mundane, between Yisrael and the nations, between the seventh day and the six days of work."</span> Since the illumination of Shabbos can only be drawn through Yisrael — on the contrary, <span class="sq">"a gentile who observes Shabbos is liable to death"</span> — it is an excess of light that causes death.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is what our Sages established in the Shabbos prayer: <span class="sq">"Therefore all His creatures shall glorify and bless G-d… who bequeaths rest to His people Yisrael in His holiness on Shabbos Kodesh."</span> For on Shabbos, rest and repose is drawn to all creatures — through which is their entire sustenance. As it is written: <span class="v">"And the heavens and the earth were completed… And Elokim completed on the seventh day His work"</span> — and our Sages expounded: <span class="sq">"What was the world lacking? Rest. Shabbos came, rest came."</span> But all this vitality and rest drawn on Shabbos upon all creatures — this is through Yisrael keeping Shabbos. This is <span class="vs">(Shemos 31)</span>: <span class="v">"And the children of Yisrael shall keep the Shabbos… between Me and the children of Yisrael it is a sign"</span> — the secret of Shabbos holiness is only between Him and Yisrael, and <span class="kt">nothing interposes between them</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"and in His joy no stranger shall mingle"</span> <span class="vs">(Mishlei 14)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore <em>Milah</em> overrides Shabbos — for only through <em>Milah</em> is one formed into <em>Adam</em>, a Jewish man with a share in drawing Shabbos holiness into this world. And this is precisely <span class="kt">pikuach nefesh overriding Shabbos</span> — when one of Yisrael is lacking from the world, the drawing of Shabbos holiness is diminished. Therefore <span class="kt"><em>Milah</em> and Shabbos are <em>trein sahadin</em></span> — both depend on each other. It is impossible to draw Shabbos holiness except through Yisrael sealed with the holy seal of <em>Milah</em>; and it is impossible to endure in the holiness of Yisrael except through Shabbos. The essential holiness is the <em>Bris</em>, but few merit this in completeness. The sustenance of most of Yisrael — who have damaged this as they have — is through <em>Milah</em> and Shabbos. Through <em>Milah</em>, all Yisrael are called <span class="sq">"Tzadikim"</span> (as Rabeinu z"l wrote in the Torah <em>"Tzivisa Tzedek,"</em> siman 23). Through Shabbos, one merits teshuvah. On weekdays, the two witnesses are <em>Tefillin</em> and <em>Milah</em> — for on weekdays, Shabbos holiness is drawn through binding the <em>Tefillin</em>. But on Shabbos itself, <em>Tefillin</em> are forbidden — for through Shabbos holiness itself one is included and bound to Him.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 29 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 29 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Due to the immense stature of Shabbos holiness, even the <span class="kt">mitzvah of Shofar on Rosh HaShanah is pushed aside</span> on account of Shabbos <em>shvus</em> — when Rosh HaShanah falls on Shabbos, we do not blow the Shofar, lest one carry it four <em>amos</em> in the public domain <span class="vs">(Rosh HaShanah 29b)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
For on Shabbos it is forbidden to carry from the public domain to the private domain or vice versa. The <em>Tanna</em> began Maseches Shabbos with <em>hotza'ah</em> specifically — <span class="sq">"because it was dear to him, he placed it first."</span> This is: <span class="v">"The goings-out of Shabbos are two that are four inside, and two that are four outside. How so? The poor man stands outside and the householder inside."</span> Inside and outside — the aspect of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. Within each there is <em>ratzo va'shov</em>. The poor man and householder are the Rav and <em>talmid</em> — Moshe and Yehoshua, the upper and lower points. Within the householder (Tzadik/Moshe) himself, there is <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> — inner <em>avodos</em> (Torah and prayer) and outer <em>avodos</em> (eating, speaking with people) — each with <em>ratzo va'shov</em>. And the <em>nafik</em> of the Tzadik is far higher than the <em>ayeil</em> of the rest of the world. Likewise among ordinary people, each has <em>ayeil</em> (Torah and prayer) and <em>nafik</em> (commerce, eating), each requiring <em>ratzo va'shov</em> — not to enter or exit beyond the boundary. Therefore: two that are four inside, two that are four outside.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 30 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 30 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
All the <span class="kt">thirty-nine <em>melachos</em></span> of the weekdays are <em>berurim</em> — refining holiness from outside to inside. The essential holiness is inside, in the aspect of <em>ayeil</em>; but one must be expert in <em>nafik</em> as well, for this is why a person comes to this world — for the test and the choice. One is compelled to go outside to attend to bodily needs, where the essential test lies. One must be expert there, withstand the test, refine what needs refining, and bring it from outside to inside. This is the service of every person in this world. Therefore the <em>Tanna</em> placed <em>hotza'ah</em> first — all <em>melachos</em> are included in the prohibition of carrying from domain to domain. All <em>melachos</em> are <em>berurim</em> from the public domain to <span class="sq">"the domain of the Singular One of the world,"</span> as the Zohar says. On Shabbos, carrying is forbidden — for on Shabbos there is no <em>birur</em>. Shabbos holiness is the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — the essential assistance of <span class="sq">"one who comes to purify — he is assisted: they say to him, Wait!"</span> All transgressions come from not waiting. Adam HaRishon sinned because he did not wait <span class="vs">(Beraishis Rabbah 21:7)</span> — <span class="sq">"Who will uncover the dust from your eyes, Adam HaRishon"</span> etc. The essential damage is through <span class="kt">haste</span> — <span class="v">"and one who rushes with his feet sins"</span> <span class="vs">(Mishlei 19)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
All this stems from the damage to the expertise — not waiting and settling one's mind properly. From this comes <span class="sq">"he peered and was stricken,"</span> <span class="sq">"he peered and died"</span> — the damage of the great Tzadikim who damaged the expertise of <em>ratzo va'shov</em>, of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> — until there are those in the aspect of <em>Acher</em> who <span class="sq">"cut the saplings,"</span> <em>Rachmana litzlan</em>. Only <span class="kt">Rabbi Akiva entered in peace and exited in peace</span> — expert in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. When Shabbos arrives, it is forbidden to carry in or out — for then there is no <em>birur</em>, but the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — rest and repose upon all the <em>berurim</em>. Every person must rest and not leave his place: <span class="v">"Let every man remain in his place; let no man go out from his place"</span> <span class="vs">(Shemos 16)</span>. One must draw upon oneself the holiness of Shabbos — <span class="sq">"repose of tranquility and quietude"</span> — to draw settling of mind, the expertise, the <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — the essential ways of teshuvah rectified on Shabbos.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 31 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 31 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
On <span class="kt">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the essential teshuvah, for it is the first of the Ten Days of Teshuvah. For then Adam HaRishon was created and called <em>Adam</em> — in order that he merit being included in the aspect of <em>Adam</em> mentioned above. He damaged this through his sin, and the rectification is through teshuvah — which one must engage in every year from Rosh HaShanah until Shemini Atzeres, all to rectify the aspect of <em>Adam</em>. The essential teshuvah is through the blowing of the Shofar, as it is written: <span class="sq">"Awake, you sleepers, from your slumber"</span> etc. Therefore they blow <em>TaRaT</em> — a <em>tekiah</em> at the beginning and a <em>tekiah</em> at the end, the aspect of the upper point and the lower point — and a <em>teru'ah</em> in the middle (or <em>teru'ah</em> and <em>shevarim</em> out of doubt), in which are the changes of voices of groaning and wailing. This is the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> — the aspect of changes of colors, that one's face changes to many colors through the shame and spilling of blood, which is the essential teshuvah, as is written there. Therefore Rosh HaShanah is on <em>Rosh Chodesh</em> — for then is the essential rectification of the moon, the aspect of Yehoshua, rectified through teshuvah — whereby the sun illuminates the moon, Moshe illuminates Yehoshua, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore, <span class="kt">when Rosh HaShanah falls on Shabbos we do not blow the Shofar</span> — lest one carry it four <em>amos</em> in the public domain. For all teshuvah is through Shabbos, and the main thing is through the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — <span class="v">"Let no man go out from his place on the day of Shabbos"</span> — not to go outside one's four <em>amos</em>. For the four <em>amos</em> each person has in the public domain are like a private domain for him; when one carries out from them, this is carrying from the private domain to the public domain — the essential prohibition of <em>melachah</em> on Shabbos, the essential damage to teshuvah, the damage to the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. Therefore, on account of this decree — lest one carry the Shofar from domain to domain — they prohibited blowing the Shofar on Shabbos entirely, outside the Mikdash. For outside the Mikdash one must be very careful not to damage the expertise. Especially now, in the time of the <em>churban</em> and the length of the exile, both collectively and individually — one must wait a great deal, as it is written <span class="vs">(Chavakuk 2)</span>: <span class="v">"If He delays, wait for Him."</span> And <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 6)</span>: <span class="v">"Therefore Hashem will wait to be gracious to you."</span> And <span class="vs">(Tzefaniah 3)</span>: <span class="v">"Therefore wait for Me, until the day I arise."</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
And likewise every individual — regarding the bitter exile of his soul, having strayed through his sins, his soul greatly distressed, desiring and yearning to return to Hashem, yet it has not been achieved — he must be very careful never to despair in any way, as has been explained countless times. For one must wait a great deal. And all this is drawn through the holiness of Shabbos, through which is the essential rectification of teshuvah. Therefore, on account of the concern lest one carry — which is the damage to Shabbos, the damage to the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, the damage to the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — they overrode the Shofar on Shabbos. The essential concern is that due to the preoccupation with the mitzvah of blowing the Shofar, one will go to a sage etc. and thereby carry four <em>amos</em> — specifically from the concern of haste and urgency. For every Jew is in a great rush to return to Hashem. But one who truly wishes to return in teshuvah, though he must urge himself and run with all his might to the true Tzadikim — nevertheless, he must wait. Therefore they were concerned that through the preoccupation with the mitzvah of Shofar — which is itself teshuvah — one might damage the holiness of Shabbos, which is the essential teshuvah. So they overrode the Shofar and relied on the holiness of Shabbos itself — that through Shabbos holiness alone, the ways of teshuvah will be rectified on Rosh HaShanah.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 32 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 32 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">five remembrances</span> one must recall in the blessing of <em>Ahavas Olam</em> and in <em>Krias Shema</em> — which is the <em>emunah</em> of the Unity, as our Sages said. One must remember the deed of Amalek, as it is written <span class="vs">(Devarim 25)</span>: <span class="v">"Remember what Amalek did to you on the way, when you went out from Mitzrayim"</span> — the aspect of cutting off the seed of Amalek, corresponding to the lower point — <span class="v">"Be still before Hashem"</span> — subduing the blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em>. For now all the wars fought against Amalek are only through remembrance, which is the essential mitzvah. Therefore we read Parshas <em>Zachor</em> in the Torah — a positive commandment from the Torah. And likewise one must remember every day, especially before accepting the yoke of Heaven in <em>Krias Shema</em> — to focus the thought that the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek constantly ambushes every Jew without limit. It is impossible to completely uproot him — lest free will be annulled — only in the end of ends his memory will be erased. For now, one must remember and believe in this, so that our eyes always look to Hashem to save us — to fulfill <span class="v">"Be still before Hashem"</span> and bear the spilling of blood etc. All this through the power of the holiness of Shabbos, the aspect of the holiness of the Tzadik — which one must remember always, in the aspect of <span class="v">"Remember the day of Shabbos"</span> — <span class="sq">"remember it from the first day of the week"</span> etc. (See below.)
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">remembrance of the deed of Miriam</span> — not to speak <em>lashon hara</em>. For <em>lashon hara</em> is equivalent to the three transgressions — <em>avodah zarah</em>, <em>giluy arayos</em>, and <em>shefichas damim</em> — corresponding to the three points, and it is the greatest of them all. For through <em>lashon hara</em> one separates the <em>Aluf</em> — <span class="v">"a gossip separates"</span> etc. — separating <em>Klal Yisrael</em> (the lower point) from the Tzadik (the upper point). The essential <em>lashon hara</em> is when one speaks against the Tzadik and those attached to him. And even when one speaks against any individual Jew — according to the <em>lashon hara</em>, so is accusation aroused, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, against him, to separate him from the point etc. Therefore the essential rectification is <em>shalom</em> — for this one must make oneself like a wilderness, bear the spilling of blood, and be among <span class="sq">"those who hear their disgrace and do not respond"</span> — until they return to us etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the <span class="kt">remembrance of the Exodus from Mitzrayim</span>. For the Exodus and the downfall of Amalek are one aspect — subduing the enemies and pursuers of Yisrael, who are the aspect of the evil blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em>, from which is the hold of all the seventy nations. And Yisrael were chosen from every people and tongue — primarily through the holiness of our forefathers, the seven shepherds, and primarily Moshe Rabeinu — who sanctified themselves so greatly that they completely subdued the blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em> and all the desires and evil traits drawn from it. They drew the holiness to their offspring until all Yisrael are holy vis-à-vis the seventy nations — through receiving the Torah, which is preserved for us to sanctify ourselves in eating and drinking and in matters of marriage according to the Torah. Through this they subdue the blood in the <em>chalal has'mali</em>, through which we went out from Mitzrayim with miracles and wonders — because they had already been somewhat sanctified, through the merit of the forefathers and the merit of the Torah they would receive.
</p>

<p class="p">
We must remember the Exodus daily — to strengthen our <em>emunah</em> and look forward to salvation always. Just as He took us out of Mitzrayim with such awesome signs and wonders, so may we merit to subdue all enemies. The main one is Amalek who includes them all — <span class="v">"Amalek is the first of the nations"</span> <span class="vs">(Bamidbar 24)</span>. One must remember well that they have already uprooted him many times — in the days of Moshe and Yehoshua and David and Mordechai — but he is such a strong <em>kelipah</em> that Hashem Himself fights him until the generation of Mashiach, as it is written: <span class="v">"A war for Hashem with Amalek from generation to generation."</span> All this because the power of free will has great force, and He does not wish to annul free will. Every Jew is obligated to fight him — the battle of the <em>yetzer</em> in this world, the essential battle with the turbidity of the bloods from the <em>chalal has'mali</em>. But most people are of weak strength. Therefore all our strength is through the great Tzadikim in every generation who fight on our behalf, as is written in the Torah <span class="sq">"T'las nafkin mei'chad"</span> <span class="vs">(siman 58)</span>. They make known to us and draw to us the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh — through which every Jew has the power to remain standing and not fall completely into the portion of the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek, who ambushes to uproot a person entirely from both worlds, <em>chas v'shalom</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
All of Amalek's war is in many aspects — he descends and entices, intensifying the evil bloods and introducing many evil thoughts into the heart, which are the essential evil inclinations — and he ascends and accuses, and through the accusations his power intensifies further, <em>chas v'shalom</em>. And not content with this, he spreads himself with awesome stratagems to <span class="kt">increase <em>machlokes</em> against the true Tzadik</span> who is mighty in strength — until he introduces <em>ti'na</em> even into the hearts of great Tzadikim, to divide their hearts from him by introducing many difficulties against him — until through this many of Yisrael distance themselves from him. And this is the worst of all.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 33 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 33 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Through all the above, you will understand on your own all the <span class="kt">five remembrances</span> one must recall in <em>Krias Shema</em> and its blessings. The remembrance of the deed of Amalek — the essential totality of the <em>kelipos</em> that wishes to distance from Hashem, even introducing heresies into the heart, <em>Rachmana litzlan</em>. Therefore one must remember this well. For now we have no power to fight him physically — only to remember well the deed of Amalek, so we understand that all the evil thoughts and evil desires are all the deeds of Amalek. But we have the power to subdue him — even those who consider themselves very weak — only one must remember the day of Shabbos, the aspect of the power of the true Tzadik. And likewise one must remember <em>Matan Torah</em>, the aspect of Shabbos on which the Torah was given — for through the holy Torah that Moshe drew for us, and through the <em>chidushei Torah</em> of the true Tzadikim in every generation, they subdue him. And most of all, one must remember the Exodus from Mitzrayim — a positive commandment to remember every day aloud — to remember and take to heart that Hashem has already helped us greatly, and we have already gone out from many very powerful <em>kelipos</em> — the aspect of the Exodus from Mitzrayim. Through this our hope and strength will be fortified to prevail against him with the power of the Tzadik — until it will be fulfilled: <span class="v">"As in the days of your going out from Mitzrayim, I will show him wonders."</span> For this reason we mention the Exodus extensively in the prayer, in <em>Emes V'yatziv</em> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
But one must also remember the deed of Miriam — to be careful of <em>lashon hara</em>. For the greatest damage is through <em>lashon hara</em> — through which one distances, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, from the true Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe, through whom comes all the strengthening in this <span class="kt">long war</span>. As is written in all the <em>mussar</em> books — all the physical wars between kings are called a <span class="sq">"small war"</span> compared to the war a person has with himself, which is called a <span class="sq">"long war."</span> This is the essential war of Amalek in every generation — collectively and individually, with every person, at every time. And most of Yisrael have no power to stand in his war except through the power of the Tzadik. Through <em>lashon hara</em> one is separated from him — therefore he is called a <span class="sq">"gossip who separates the chief [<em>aluf</em>]."</span> Therefore they warned us to remember the deed of Miriam — to teach that even though they were awesome Tzadikim, nevertheless Aharon and Miriam erred in this, that they questioned Moshe. All the more so that it is forbidden for the rest of the people to question, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, the true Tzadik — even if one hears from other Tzadikim some difficulty against him, all the more so etc.
</p>

<div class="rp">Heads of Chapters:</div>

<!-- ══════════ Section 34 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 34 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore Rosh HaShanah is called <span class="kt">Yom HaZikaron</span> [the Day of Remembrance]. For then is the first of the Ten Days of Teshuvah, and the essential teshuvah is through remembrance — remembering Hashem and the World to Come, prevailing over whatever each person must prevail over. This is the aspect of remembering Shabbos, which is the aspect of the World to Come — through which one subdues the aspect of Amalek etc. Likewise all the remembrances are included in Rosh HaShanah, which is the first of the Ten Days of Teshuvah, comprised of all of them. For in it is the aspect of the <em>beginning</em> of teshuvah, which is very difficult, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Mechilta, Parshas Yisro)</span>: <span class="sq">"All beginnings are difficult."</span> Therefore Rosh HaShanah is called Yom HaZikaron.
</p>

<p class="p">
For Amalek is the totality of the <em>kelipos</em>, the totality of the exile of the seventy nations, who wish to cause His Name to be forgotten from the world, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, as it is written <span class="vs">(see Yirmiyahu 23)</span>: <span class="v">"who plan to make [the people] forget Your Name."</span> Therefore they devise plans against Yisrael, as it is written <span class="vs">(Tehillim 83)</span>: <span class="v">"Against Your people they conspire cunningly… so that the name of Yisrael will no longer be remembered. They have conspired with one heart against You; they cut a covenant"</span> etc. Therefore one must remember the deed of Amalek — to know that all manner of evil thoughts, whether of fantasies and desires, or of difficulties and heresies — all are the aspect of Amalek. And one must remember that the ancient Tzadikim already uprooted him, yet he still intensifies and spreads. But Hashem will uproot him completely — <span class="v">"A war for Hashem with Amalek from generation to generation"</span> — in every generation Hashem sends great Tzadikim who draw the magnification of His Name and His remembrance in the world, in the aspect of <span class="v">"This is My Name forever, and this is My remembrance from generation to generation"</span> <span class="vs">(Shemos 3)</span>. For through the erasure of Amalek from generation to generation, His remembrance is maintained from generation to generation. And this is <span class="vs">(Pesachim 50)</span>: <span class="sq">"'This is My Name' and 'this is My remembrance'… Not as I am written am I read"</span> etc. — this is the aspect of the upper point and the lower point. For the Name <em>Havayah</em> — the aspect of <span class="v">"This is My Name forever"</span> — is the upper point, the aspect of <span class="v">"the glory of G-d is to conceal a matter."</span> And the Name <em>Adnus</em> — the aspect of <span class="v">"this is My remembrance"</span> — is the lower point, the aspect of the revelation of His <em>malchus</em>. When these two Names are included and unified together — the lower point and the upper point — through this the aspect of <em>Adam</em> is formed.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the five remembrances mentioned above: the remembrance of the deed of Amalek and the Exodus from Mitzrayim — the aspect of the lower point. Shabbos — the aspect of the upper point, comprised of all of them, the aspect of Moshe etc. The remembrance of <em>Matan Torah</em> — the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em>, for the essential binding and drawing of vitality from the upper point to the lower point is through the Torah, which is the <em>amuda d'emtza'isa</em> [middle pillar]. And one must also remember the deed of Miriam — to be very careful of <em>lashon hara</em> and <em>machlokes</em>, which is the opposite of all this and ruins everything, <em>chas v'shalom</em> — for it is the aspect of <span class="v">"a gossip separates the chief [<em>aluf</em>],"</span> as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 35 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 35 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Pashta Munach Zarka Segol</em></span>. <em>Zarka</em> — the aspect of the lower point, the essential teshuvah to return it to its place, to the aspect of the upper point, in the aspect of <span class="sq"><em>"Zarka d'izdrikas la'asar d'isnat'lis misaman"</em></span> [the <em>zarka</em> that was thrown to the place from which it was taken], as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <em>Segol</em>, which is the upper point, as is written in the aforementioned Torah. And <em>Pashta</em> — this is the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em>, through which they are bound and included together, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <em>Munach</em> — the aspect of Shabbos, which is repose and rest. For the essential binding and inclusion is through Shabbos, which is a day of rest, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 36 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 36 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Krias HaTorah</em> on Shabbos</span>. For the essential fulfillment of the Torah is through Shabbos. For the fulfillment of the Torah is very difficult to draw into the world — <span class="v">"for the inclination of man's heart is evil from his youth"</span> — and the <em>ba'al davar</em> [Adversary] incites every person greatly to sin, <em>chas v'shalom</em>. Therefore many have stumbled as they have stumbled — Hashem have mercy from now on. For this reason the angels protested greatly about the creation of man, saying <span class="sq">"What is man…?"</span> — <span class="sq">"he is destined to sin before You"</span> etc. And likewise at <em>Matan Torah</em> they protested the giving of the Torah to Yisrael <span class="vs">(Shabbos 88b)</span> — all because they saw that many would transgress the Torah and few would merit fulfilling it completely. But Hashem did not listen to them and said: <span class="sq">"For nothing was I called merciful and gracious?"</span> — for He saw that there would be Tzadikim in every generation who would fulfill the Torah in completeness, and they would return Yisrael in teshuvah until in the end the complete redemption will come through Mashiach Tzidkeinu. Therefore He created man and gave the Torah to Yisrael. It emerges that the essential <em>Matan Torah</em> was through <span class="kt">teshuvah that preceded the world</span> — Hashem preceded the remedy to the affliction, which is teshuvah. Even if Yisrael sin, He has already prepared for them the remedy of teshuvah. But the essential way of teshuvah is drawn through the holiness of Shabbos, which the true Tzadikim draw, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore we <span class="kt">read the Torah on Shabbos more than any other time of the year</span> — reading all seven <em>aliyos</em> and completing the entire Torah from <em>sidra</em> to <em>sidra</em> each Shabbos until completing it on Simchas Torah. This shows that the essential drawing of the Torah into the world — that we merit to fulfill it, which is the main thing — is through Shabbos, the aspect of teshuvah, through which the essential fulfillment of the Torah comes. Therefore we read seven <em>aliyos</em> on Shabbos, corresponding to the <span class="kt">seven shepherds</span> from Avraham to David — whose essential one is Moshe, who gave us the Torah. For all the seven shepherds drew the Torah. The beginning was from Avraham, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Yoma 28b)</span>: <span class="sq">"Avraham fulfilled the entire Torah"</span> etc. — and from him to Yitzchak and Yaakov etc., until it was given through Moshe Rabeinu. But the essential place of the Torah is in Eretz Yisrael and the Beis HaMikdash, as it is written <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 2)</span>: <span class="v">"For from Tziyon shall go forth Torah."</span> Therefore the completion of the fulfillment of the Torah is through David, the seventh of the seven shepherds — for he labored and found the place of the Beis HaMikdash and engaged in its building, and it is named after him: <span class="sq">"Beis David."</span> Therefore we read seven <em>aliyos</em> on Shabbos, the aspect of the seven shepherds — for the essential fulfillment of the Torah drawn through the seven shepherds is through Shabbos, the aspect of teshuvah.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 37 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 37 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is the aspect of the reading of the <span class="kt">Maftir in the Nevi'im</span>. For all the Nevi'im are in the aspect of <em>talmidim</em> of Moshe Rabeinu, who is the master of all the Nevi'im. Therefore they are all included in Yehoshua — the first book of the Nevi'im — for he was the first <em>talmid</em>-prophet of Moshe, <span class="v">"who did not depart from the Tent."</span> All the Nevi'im received through him, as they said <span class="vs">(Avos 1)</span>: <span class="sq">"Moshe received Torah from Sinai and transmitted it to Yehoshua, and Yehoshua to the Elders, and the Elders to the Nevi'im"</span> etc. Yehoshua is the aspect of the lower point, receiving from the upper point which is Moshe — through whom specifically Moshe illuminates the Torah to Yisrael. Therefore all the seven shepherds — whose essential one is Moshe, through whom the Torah is drawn — all their illumination of the Torah to Yisrael is through the aspect of Yehoshua, the lower point, the aspect of the <em>chirik</em> of <em>S"G</em>, the aspect of <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — as is explained in the aforementioned Torah. For the essential thing is to cut off the seed of Amalek, from whom comes the seething of the bloods in the <em>chalal has'mali</em>, from which come all sins and damages, <em>Rachmana litzlan</em>. This war is waged by Moshe — the true Rav of the generation — through the aspect of Yehoshua the <em>talmid</em>. For specifically Yehoshua pertains to this, as it is written: <span class="v">"Go out and fight with Amalek."</span> Through this specifically, the upper point illuminates the lower point and the way of teshuvah is drawn into the world. Therefore we read the Nevi'im for <em>Maftir</em>, for all the Nevi'im are in the aspect of Yehoshua — and we read them after <em>Krias HaTorah</em>, for the essential fulfillment of the Torah is through the aspect of Yehoshua the <em>talmid</em>, the lower point, through whom the Tzadik (the aspect of Moshe) draws the way of teshuvah into the world — through the holiness of Shabbos.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore we mention in the <span class="kt">blessings of the <em>Haftarah</em></span> the sprouting of the horn of Mashiach — that in the end Hashem will finish and all Yisrael will merit fulfilling the Torah through Mashiach Tzidkeinu, as is stated in those blessings: <span class="sq">"Faithful are You, Hashem our G-d, and not a single word of Your words shall return empty… for in Your holy Name You swore to him that his light shall never be extinguished"</span> etc. For the essential fulfillment of the Torah — which is the sprouting of the horn of Mashiach — is through the aspect of the Nevi'im, the aspect of Yehoshua, the lower point, through which the Tzadik (the aspect of Moshe) draws the way of teshuvah into the world.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is what our Sages said — that they instituted reading the Nevi'im for <em>Maftir</em> because once the wicked kingdom decreed that the Torah should not be read, and they read from the Nevi'im instead; afterwards, when the decree was annulled, they retained the practice of reading the Nevi'im for all generations. That is: even when the wicked kingdom wishes to nullify the Torah from Yisrael through their great provocations — nevertheless, the Torah is sustained through the Nevi'im. Therefore they did not abolish this practice of reading the Nevi'im after <em>Krias HaTorah</em> — to show and make known that the essential fulfillment of the Torah is through the Nevi'im, the aspect of Yehoshua, the aspect of the <em>talmidim</em> of the true Tzadikim, through whom the essential drawing of the way of teshuvah comes — through which the Torah is fulfilled, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 38 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 38 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore before Rosh HaShanah, Yom Kippur, and the Ten Days of Teshuvah is the <span class="kt">month of Elul</span>, in which Yisrael are aroused in teshuvah. For then they are engaged — through the power of the Tzadikim — in making the road for teshuvah, which is the aspect of <span class="v">"Who makes a way in the sea and a path in mighty waters"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 43)</span> — which is the secret of the <em>kavanos</em> of Elul, as mentioned above. Its essential aspect is the expertise in halachah mentioned above — the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven, there You are; and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> etc. For through this one merits Rosh HaShanah, Yom Kippur, and the Ten Days of Teshuvah, in which is the essential teshuvah. For it is impossible to merit teshuvah except through this path — when one accepts upon himself that however things may be, he will strengthen himself in Hashem always, in the aspect of <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — then specifically he will merit teshuvah. For there are many ascents and descents that every person must pass through, which cannot be explained in writing. Especially one who has already stumbled as he has stumbled — therefore, if he wishes to have compassion on his life and better his end, he must go on his hands and knees to receive this path of the expertise in halachah mentioned above. And then he will merit a good end forever.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 39 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 39 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And now — how beautiful and how pleasant is the order that our Sages established for us: <span class="v">"everything beautiful in its time"</span> <span class="vs">(Koheles 3)</span>! For immediately on <span class="kt">Simchas Torah</span>, when we conclude the Torah with the passing of Moshe — <span class="v">"and Yehoshua was full of the spirit of wisdom"</span> etc. (as above, section 25) — we begin <em>Beraishis</em> and read until the <em>parshah</em> of the holiness of Shabbos, which is <em>Vayechulu</em>, as mentioned above. For the essential <em>avodah</em> from Elul until now is to draw the way of teshuvah into the world — primarily through Shabbos, which the Tzadikim — the aspect of Moshe who illuminates and makes known through the aspect of Yehoshua — make known to us. Therefore we immediately read the <em>Haftarah</em> from the beginning of the Book of Yehoshua, which speaks of the passing of Moshe and Yehoshua's need to strengthen himself, as it is written there <span class="vs">(Yehoshua 1)</span>: <span class="v">"Moshe My servant has died — now arise, cross over"</span> etc., and they conclude with: <span class="v">"Only be strong and courageous."</span> For this is the essential rectification and hope — that Moshe drew such a rectification that even after his passing he continues to draw the way of teshuvah into the world through the aspect of Yehoshua, through which they conquer Eretz Yisrael etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this we read every single year. For <span class="v">"His dominion is in every generation"</span> — each year this aspect is accomplished anew, beginning the Torah from <em>sidra</em> to <em>sidra</em> each Shabbos until completing it at the conclusion of the seal within the seal of Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur, which is on Shemini Atzeres and Simchas Torah. And it is all in order to illuminate and draw the holiness of Shabbos, which is the way of teshuvah in the world. For <span class="kt">I heard from his holy mouth, <em>zichrono livrachah</em></span>, after he said the aforementioned Torah, that <span class="sq">wherever there is a true Rav and a <em>talmid</em> who learns from him, there is formed this aspect of Moshe and Yehoshua and the <em>Ohel Mo'ed</em> — through which the essential drawing of the way of teshuvah into the world comes about.</span> And in this they are engaged from Elul until Shemini Atzeres, and then they read the <em>Haftarah</em> from Yehoshua — which is the first <em>Haftarah</em>, for all the <em>Haftaros</em> from the Nevi'im are in the aspect of Yehoshua, the first book of the Nevi'im. For Yehoshua is the lower point, through whom the aspect of Moshe illuminates in every generation, every year, the way of teshuvah into the world, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 40 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 40 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
It has been explained throughout all the above that the essential rectification is <span class="kt">Shabbos</span> — which is a <span class="sq">"good gift"</span> etc. And the main thing: through Shabbos everyone will merit teshuvah, through which the redemption will come. Therefore one must be very careful to honor Shabbos and remember it always. For Shabbos is the <em>tachlis</em> — the aspect of <span class="sq">"the purpose of heaven and earth"</span> etc. This is why they conclude the <span class="kt"><em>Haftarah</em> of Yom Kippur</span> — which speaks of teshuvah — with the keeping of Shabbos, as it is written there <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 58)</span>: <span class="v">"If you restrain your foot because of Shabbos… then you shall delight upon Hashem"</span> etc. For the essential teshuvah is on Yom Kippur, and even Yom Kippur receives from Shabbos. For Shabbos is the <span class="sq">"head of all the sacred times,"</span> as we say, <span class="sq">"and You blessed it above all the times"</span> — for all the teshuvah rectification of Yom Kippur is through Shabbos. Therefore they conclude the <em>Haftarah</em> of Yom Kippur with the keeping of Shabbos.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 41 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 41 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Through this you will see <span class="kt">wonders in the Torah</span> — how everything is arranged in a wondrous order. For this reason Moshe Rabeinu placed the <em>parshah</em> of keeping Shabbos <em>before</em> the <em>parshah</em> of the sin of the Golden Calf, as is evident in Parshas <em>Ki Sisa</em> — which speaks of the <em>Mishkan</em>, then: <span class="v">"But My Shabbasos you shall keep… and the children of Yisrael shall keep the Shabbos… an eternal covenant between Me and the children of Yisrael"</span> — and immediately afterward begins speaking of the sin of the Calf. This is to reveal and make known the kindnesses of Hashem and His wonders: that in His wondrous kindness He always <span class="kt">precedes the remedy to the affliction</span>, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Megillah 13b)</span>. Before this evil and bitter deed of the sin of the Golden Calf — through which they caused damage for all generations and brought about all the <em>churbanos</em>, for they drew the <em>yetzer hara</em> back upon themselves, from which all the damages come even now — He preceded and made known to us in His Torah the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh, placing it first, to show that He has already preceded the rectification and the remedy, which is the holiness of Shabbos that we receive from the true Tzadikim, the aspect of Moshe. Through this everything will be rectified, for through this the way of teshuvah is drawn into the world. Therefore the <span class="kt">thousand lights</span> that were withdrawn through the sin of the Golden Calf are returned to Moshe on Shabbos, and Moshe returns them to Yisrael, as is written in the <em>kavanos</em> — for the essential rectification of the sin of the Golden Calf is through Shabbos.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore at the entrance of Shabbos we say <span class="kt"><em>Mizmor Shir L'Yom HaShabbos</em></span> — the psalm that Adam HaRishon said at the entrance of Shabbos, close to his sin, as our Sages said — for <span class="sq">"Shabbos protected him."</span> And this is the aspect of what is written in the tale of <span class="kt">the Two Children Who Were Exchanged</span> — that when one is pursued from Gan Eden, one must station oneself by Shabbos, as is written there, and as brought above. Therefore he said there <span class="vs">(Tehillim 92)</span>: <span class="v">"How great are Your works, Hashem; exceedingly deep are Your thoughts. A boorish man does not know… But You are exalted forever, Hashem."</span> Rashi explains: <span class="sq">"Forever Your hand is on high."</span> That is — he saw immediately that even though the <em>Samech-Mem</em> and his forces provoked so much that they caused him to sin on the very first day — from which would result everything that would result — nevertheless, in His mercy Hashem rested <em>Ruach HaKodesh</em> upon him, and he immediately said <em>Mizmor Shir L'Yom HaShabbos</em> and concluded: <span class="v">"But You are exalted forever, Hashem"</span> — for forever Your hand is on high. For through the power of Shabbos holiness, His G-dliness will be revealed always — through the Tzadikim — even in <em>ikvesa d'Meshicha</em>, even in these last generations where the concealment within the concealment has intensified without end. Nevertheless, the way of teshuvah will continue to be drawn into the world — the aspect of <span class="v">"If I ascend to heaven… and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — through the holiness of Shabbos.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is also what we say afterward in the psalm <span class="v">"Hashem has reigned, He has donned grandeur"</span> etc. <span class="vs">(Tehillim 93)</span>: <span class="v">"More than the voices of many waters, mightier than the breakers of the sea — mighty on high is Hashem."</span> For even though the many waters and mighty breakers of the sea overwhelm every person so greatly — in the aspect of <span class="v">"all Your breakers and waves have passed over me"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 42)</span> — nevertheless, mightier than all of them is Hashem on high. He will finish everything according to His will and bring everything to the true, eternal, good end — for He has already preceded and given Yisrael the holiness of Shabbos. Therefore we say this at the entrance of Shabbos.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore, even one who has sinned greatly, <em>chas v'shalom</em> — as long as he is not a <em>mumar</em> to anger [Hashem] or to <span class="kt">desecrate Shabbos</span>, he is included in <em>Klal Yisrael</em> etc. But a <em>mumar</em> to desecrate Shabbos is like a <em>mumar</em> to the entire Torah and leaves the category of Yisrael. For the essential sustaining of the holiness of Yisrael is through Shabbos, as mentioned above.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 42 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 42 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore, in everything that passes over a person, one must <span class="kt">remember Shabbos always</span>. Especially regarding <em>parnasah</em> — for one must sanctify all the activities and commerce, which are included in the thirty-nine <em>melachos</em>, with the holiness of Shabbos, as Rabeinu z"l wrote <span class="vs">(siman 38)</span>. For one must remember that the essential blessing of <em>parnasah</em> is from Shabbos — <span class="sq">"from which all six days are blessed."</span> And likewise when thoughts pursue a person greatly — one must station oneself by Shabbos, which is rest and repose. For the essential rectification of thought is to be <span class="sq">"sit and do not act"</span> — even in thought, as is written elsewhere — and to nullify oneself in the holiness of Shabbos and direct one's thought into the Torah that was given on Shabbos, from which all <em>chidushei Torah</em> are drawn. And likewise regarding jealousy, hatred, and <em>machlokes</em> between a person and his fellow or between husband and wife — which is very common and very damaging as is known — one must remind oneself of the holiness of Shabbos, which is <em>shalom</em>, the aspect of <span class="sq">"Shabbos Shalom."</span> And in everything that passes over a person each and every day, one must flee in thought to the holiness of Shabbos — all through the power of the Tzadikim who make known to us the holiness of Shabbos.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 43 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 43 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is: <span class="v">"If you restrain your foot because of Shabbos"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 58)</span> — for the main thing is to rectify the aspect of the <span class="kt"><em>raglayim</em> [feet]</span>, which are the vessels of walking, to walk in the way of the Torah, in the ways of teshuvah mentioned above — in the aspect of <span class="v">"my foot has stood on a straight path"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 26)</span>. For the essential <em>birur</em> is in the aspect of the <em>raglayim</em>, for there is all their [the <em>kelipos</em>'] hold — in the aspect of <span class="v">"her feet descend"</span> etc. <span class="vs">(Mishlei 5)</span>. For this is the aspect of the intensity of the provocation that the <em>ba'al davar</em> provokes now, in these generations, both collectively and individually. For now it is <span class="kt"><em>ikvesa d'Meshicha</em></span> — the aspect of the <em>raglayim</em>, where the hold of the <em>Sitra Achra</em> and the <em>kelipos</em> is very great. Therefore one must know that now, in these generations, when a person exerts himself with even a slight movement to return to Hashem, it is exceedingly precious to Hashem — more than the great <em>avodos</em> of the earlier generations, as is brought in the writings of the Arizal and in the words of the Ba'al Shem Tov z"l, and especially from the words of Admo"r z"l. Therefore the essential end of the redemption is <span class="sq">"when the feet reach the feet"</span> — and there will be fulfilled: <span class="v">"And His feet shall stand on that day"</span> etc. — as is brought extensively in the writings of the Arizal. And all the rectification is through Shabbos, in the aspect of <span class="v">"If you restrain your foot because of Shabbos"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
For this world as a whole is the aspect of the <em>raglayim</em> — <span class="v">"and the earth is My footstool"</span> — the aspect of the lower point, which is the aspect of silence and waiting: <span class="v">"Be still before Hashem"</span>… <span class="v">"Only to G-d is my soul silent"</span>… <span class="v">"It is good to wait in stillness for the salvation of Hashem"</span> — meaning silence and waiting. For in this world one must only <span class="kt">wait</span>. For it is a passing shadow, a dissipating cloud, a blowing wind. People must pass through it for the test and the refining. The main thing is to stand in the test and wait in all things — not to chase any superfluous desire. What one truly needs — one should have trust and always wait for Hashem's salvation. Through this one merits sanctifying all the activities of the six weekdays, connecting them to Shabbos — whereby the aspect of <em>Adam</em> to sit upon the Throne is formed. For through the stillness and silence, one sanctifies the weekday activities, drawing through the six weekdays the aspect of the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em> — binding oneself to the upper point, the <em>Kisei d'miskasya</em>, from which the essential Shabbos holiness derives. For Shabbos in general is the upper point, and the world in general is the lower point. Through sanctifying the weekdays via stillness and silence, one draws through the six weekdays — the <em>Vav</em> — from the upper point to the lower point, forming the aspect of <em>Adam</em>, which is the way of teshuvah.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is: <span class="v">"If you restrain your foot because of Shabbos, from pursuing your affairs on My holy day"</span> — for all the resting on Shabbos from pursuing one's affairs is in the aspect of <span class="v">"restrain your foot because of Shabbos,"</span> for all the <em>berurim</em> are in the <em>raglayim</em>, and therefore any pursuit of one's affairs is forbidden on this holy and awesome day. And this is: <span class="v">"And you shall call Shabbos a delight"</span> — for the essential Shabbos holiness is specifically to fulfill <span class="kt"><em>Oneg Shabbos</em></span>, which comprises the <span class="kt">three meals of Shabbos</span>, which are the aspect of <em>Eden</em>, <em>Nahar</em>, <em>Gan</em> — the three mitzvos mentioned above, the three points, which are the way of teshuvah. And this is: <span class="v">"And to the holy one of Hashem, honored — and you shall honor it from doing your ways"</span> — the rectification of <em>kavod</em>, to merit Divine honor through the ways of teshuvah, as is explained in the aforementioned Torah on this verse.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is: <span class="v">"From pursuing your affairs and speaking words"</span> — <span class="sq">"that your speech on Shabbos should not be like your speech on a weekday"</span> <span class="vs">(Shabbos 113b)</span>. For in truth the essential <span class="kt">elevation of speech</span> is on Shabbos. Then the <em>raglayim</em> ascend — which are the aspect of speech, in the aspect of <span class="v">"righteousness you shall speak"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 58)</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"righteousness calls him to his foot"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 41)</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"righteousness shall walk before him"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 85)</span>. For speech is the aspect of <em>Malchus-Peh</em> [Kingship-Mouth], whose essential ascent is on Shabbos, <em>Malchesa</em> [the Queen]. During the six weekdays — this world — one must only be silent, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Chullin 89)</span> on the verse <span class="v">"Do you indeed speak righteousness in silence?"</span>: <span class="sq">"What should a person do in this world? Make himself like a mute."</span> For it is forbidden to speak any words of worldly affairs — only words of Torah and prayer. As they said: <span class="sq">"Perhaps even words of Torah [should be silent]? The verse teaches: 'Righteousness you shall speak.'"</span> And as they said <span class="vs">(Yoma 19b)</span>: <span class="sq">"And you shall speak of them — and not of idle matters."</span> But one cannot survive without speech; one must speak about worldly needs. Only, one must bind all the words to the Torah — that all one's words in commerce and the like be for the eternal purpose, as Admo"r z"l wrote <span class="vs">(siman 29)</span>. But on Shabbos, even this is forbidden — for it is forbidden to engage in commerce or any <em>melachah</em>; even speech of weekday affairs is forbidden, even with truly holy intent. For even the <em>Mishkan</em> construction is pushed aside before Shabbos — all the more so all other commerce, even if conducted faithfully like the work of the <em>Mishkan</em>, as Rabeinu z"l wrote <span class="vs">(siman 11)</span>. On Shabbos, only <span class="kt">speech of Shabbos</span> is permitted: words of Torah and prayer and praises and thanks to Hashem, and to sing <em>zemiros</em> of Shabbos, to thank Hashem with a full heart in great and immense joy — that He gave us in His compassion this good gift that was hidden in His treasure house, which is the holiness of Shabbos. For Shabbos is above speech — all words are drawn from Shabbos. Shabbos is the aspect of the supernal silence that is above speech, the aspect of <span class="sq">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — the aspect of <em>Keser</em>, the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> For the root of speech is in thought, which is the aspect of silence. Speech is the aspect of <em>Chochmah</em> — <span class="v">"for Hashem gives wisdom; from His mouth"</span> etc. <span class="vs">(Mishlei 2)</span> — and its root is <em>Keser</em>, silence: <span class="sq">"A fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — the silence surrounds the <em>Chochmah</em>/speech like a fence. One must be silent before speaking, settling one's mind well before bringing forth the word, and likewise between one word and the next. Therefore they said <span class="vs">(Megillah 18)</span>: <span class="sq">"A word is worth a <em>sela</em>; silence is worth two."</span> For silence is above speech, its root and rectification. All this is drawn from Shabbos — <em>Keser</em>, <em>Kisei d'miskasya</em> — where is the secret of silence, the aspect of <span class="v">"wisest of the silent ones, discerning of whispers"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayahu 3)</span>. On Shabbos it is forbidden to speak any words except words of Shabbos — drawn from the supernal silence, words of settled mind, words of <em>emunah</em> to cleave through them to Hashem: <span class="v">"I will make known Your faithfulness with my mouth"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 89)</span> — <span class="sq">"And the seventh day praises and says: <em>Mizmor Shir L'Yom HaShabbos</em> — it is good to give thanks to Hashem"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is: <span class="v">"Then you shall delight <em>upon</em> Hashem"</span> — <span class="sq">"<em>upon</em> Hashem"</span> specifically, which is the <em>Oraisa d'Atika Stimah</em> [Torah of the Ancient Concealed One], the aspect of <span class="sq">"upon Hashem"</span> (as brought in the Torah <em>"LaShemesh"</em> siman 49, Likutay Part 1) — which is above the revealed Torah, where the essential teshuvah is, the aspect of Shabbos, as brought elsewhere. And this is: <span class="v">"And I will mount you upon the heights of the earth, and I will feed you the heritage of Yaakov your father"</span> — which is a <span class="kt">heritage without boundaries</span>. Through Shabbos one merits that all the <em>metzarim</em> [straits/boundaries] of holiness are nullified — through which come all the <em>churbanos</em>, collectively and individually, in the aspect of <span class="v">"all her pursuers overtook her between the straits"</span> <span class="vs">(Shabbos 118)</span>. For one who holds onto the power of Shabbos holiness — the aspect of the power of the holiness of the Tzadik, who is <span class="sq">"Shabbos of all the days"</span> — no <em>meitzar</em> can distance him from Hashem. For from everything he will return to Hashem, for he will merit the ways of teshuvah — the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, the aspect of <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span> For Yaakov's bed was complete — he nullifies the <em>kelipah</em> of Eisav and Yishmael, who are the <em>metzarim</em> of holiness that destroyed the Beis HaMikdash. For Yaakov and Moshe are considered as one — the aspect of the true Tzadik who draws the holiness of Shabbos, the ways of teshuvah. Therefore through keeping Shabbos received from the true Tzadik, one merits a heritage without boundaries — nothing can distance from Hashem, <em>chas v'shalom</em>. From everything one returns to Hashem.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 44 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 44 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
And this is what our Sages concluded there, in their teaching on the verse <span class="v">"Do you indeed [speak righteousness]"</span> cited above: <span class="sq">"Perhaps even if he becomes haughty? The verse teaches: 'With equity you shall judge the sons of Adam.'"</span> For this verse speaks of the ways of teshuvah mentioned above. The essential teshuvah is that one should <span class="kt">make oneself like a wilderness</span> and not be arrogant at all. When one hears one's disgrace, one should be still and silent and be among <span class="sq">"those who are insulted but do not insult, who hear their disgrace and do not respond"</span> — as is explained in the aforementioned Torah. This is what was stated there: <span class="sq">"What should a person do in this world? Make himself like a mute"</span> — for one must be silent and be like a mute, which is the essential teshuvah, as David said <span class="vs">(Tehillim 38)</span>: <span class="v">"And I am like a deaf man, I do not hear; like a mute who does not open his mouth."</span> To this they said: <span class="sq">"Perhaps even regarding words of Torah? The verse teaches: 'Righteousness you shall speak'"</span> — for words of Torah one certainly must speak, for through this comes the entire teshuvah rectification and rescue from all evil, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Sukkah 52b, Kiddushin 30b)</span>: <span class="sq">"If this repulsive one confronts you, drag him to the <em>Beis HaMidrash</em>."</span> And our Sages said regarding teshuvah <span class="vs">(Vayikra Rabbah 25)</span>: <span class="sq">"If one was accustomed to learning one page, let him learn two"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore they concluded: <span class="sq">"Perhaps even if he becomes haughty? The verse teaches"</span> etc. — for even if one studies Torah but becomes haughty, certainly the Torah will not be sustained in him and he will not merit teshuvah. For the essential teshuvah — which is the fulfillment of the Torah — is through <span class="kt">humility</span>: making oneself like a wilderness, as is brought at the end [of the Torah] on the teaching of Rabbah bar bar Chanah: <span class="sq">"Once we were traveling in the wilderness"</span> etc., see there. And this is: <span class="sq">"The verse teaches: 'With equity you shall judge the sons of Adam'"</span> — for one must <span class="kt">judge everyone favorably</span> and be silent before those who disgrace him, as is written there at the end on the teaching of our Sages: <span class="sq">"Judge every person favorably."</span> For the essential teshuvah rectification is through feeling one's lowliness in truth, and nevertheless strengthening oneself through the power of the true Tzadik, the aspect of Shabbos — through which one will merit to walk in the ways of teshuvah until one is shown mercy from Heaven and returns to Hashem and He will have mercy on him, amen, may it be His will.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 45 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 45 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
The essential holiness of Shabbos is <span class="kt"><em>simchah</em></span> [joy]. One must be very careful to be joyful and of good heart on Shabbos, as is written <span class="vs">(Likutay Tinyana, siman 17)</span> and as explained in the words of our Sages extensively. This is the essential rectification of the ways of teshuvah that are rectified through Shabbos holiness. For the essential teshuvah is to return one's soul to its root — in the aspect of <span class="sq"><em>"Zarka d'izdrikas la'asar d'isnat'lis misaman"</em></span> <span class="vs">(as written in siman 35, Likutay Part 1)</span> — and the root of the souls of Yisrael is the aspect of <em>simchah</em>. For their root is in the thought of the Holy One Blessed Be He — <span class="sq">"Yisrael arose in thought first"</span> — where is the source of joy, as it is written <span class="vs">(Divrei HaYamim I 16)</span>: <span class="v">"Strength and gladness are in His place"</span> — for <span class="sq">"there is no sadness before the <em>Makom</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Chagigah 5)</span>. And the essential damage of all transgressions is the aspect of <span class="kt">sadness</span>, from which all sins are drawn — their essence being the damage to the <em>Bris</em>, which is drawn from the <em>kelipah</em> called <em>Lilis</em>, named for the wailing [<em>yelalah</em>] and sadness, as is known. Therefore <span class="sq">"one transgression draws another transgression"</span> — for transgression is drawn from the contamination of the <em>Nachash</em>, the aspect of <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat of it"</span> — the opposite of the holiness of joy, which is to rejoice in Hashem: <span class="v">"I shall rejoice in Hashem"</span>, <span class="v">"Rejoice in Hashem and exult, you righteous"</span>. When one transgresses, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, sadness is drawn upon him — <span class="sq">"I shall worry because of my sin"</span> — the aspect of <em>yagon</em> and <em>anachah</em> which are the aspect of <em>Sam</em> and his female, as written in the <em>kavanos</em>. Through the intensification of sadness, further transgression is drawn — hence <span class="sq">"one transgression draws another."</span> And there would be no way to return at all. Therefore the essential teshuvah is through the great Tzadikim, who are able to <span class="kt">arouse the good points</span> in Yisrael. Even in the worst, those full of transgressions — the great Tzadikim search and find good points in them, for <span class="sq">"even the sinners of Yisrael are full of mitzvos like a pomegranate"</span> (as written in the Torah <span class="sq">"V'od me'at v'ein rasha"</span> siman 282 etc. and in many other places) — until they instill in them that they too should gladden their souls. Through this they take them out of transgressions and damages and bring them back in teshuvah.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of the holiness of Shabbos that they make known to them, as mentioned above. For Shabbos is the aspect of joy and gladness — on Shabbos all the <em>dinim</em> are nullified, as the Zohar says <span class="vs">(Terumah 135b)</span>: <span class="sq">"And all the rulers of wrath and masters of judgment — all flee and depart from her."</span> And Hashem looks then only at the good in Yisrael. Therefore whoever draws upon himself the <span class="kt">holiness of the joy of Shabbos</span> — which is drawn upon him through the power of the true Tzadikim — can merit walking in the ways of teshuvah: the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven, there You are, and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span> For although the <em>ba'al teshuvah</em> must cry out and weep greatly before Hashem and break his heart greatly — <span class="v">"a broken and crushed heart, G-d, You will not despise"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 51)</span> — nevertheless, one must be expert in this halachah, not to become more distant, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, through the very act of teshuvah. For in all the ways of holiness there is the aspect of <span class="sq">"the righteous shall walk in them"</span> and its opposite: <span class="sq">"transgressors shall stumble in them"</span> — <span class="sq">"If he merits, it becomes an elixir of life; if not…"</span> The <em>ba'al teshuvah</em> must bear humiliations and the spilling of blood in many aspects, as explained in the aforementioned Torah — that the essential teshuvah is when one hears one's disgrace and is still and silent. Included in this is also that one must bear humiliation from oneself — <span class="v">"disgrace has broken my heart"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 69)</span> — one must be ashamed of oneself from one's sins until the blood is spilled within him, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Berachos 12b)</span>: <span class="sq">"Whoever commits a transgression and is ashamed of it — all his sins are forgiven."</span> Nevertheless, one must be very expert in this halachah — <span class="kt">not to come through this to sadness and <em>marah shechorah</em></span> of the <em>Sitra Achra</em>. As Admo"r z"l warned about this, saying that <span class="sq">"a broken heart is a different matter from sadness."</span> One must be careful that sadness does not attach itself to the broken heart. Therefore he warned to exert oneself to be joyful all day, through all his holy counsels — and only at a designated time should one have a broken heart, to pour out one's conversation before Him like a child before his father etc. All this is included in the aspect of the expertise in halachah mentioned above. Therefore in truth, the essential ways of teshuvah can only be received from the great Tzadikim, who teach us the ways of teshuvah — the expertise in halachah — primarily through Shabbos holiness, the source of joy, when Hashem looks only at the good etc.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 46 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 46 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
All this is needed especially on <span class="kt">Rosh HaShanah</span>, the beginning of the year, the first day of the Ten Days of Teshuvah. Then one must draw the ways of teshuvah mentioned above. Therefore Hashem in His mercy preceded for us the holy month of <span class="kt">Elul</span>, whose secret <em>kavanos</em> are <span class="v">"Who makes a way in the sea"</span> — for in it they engage in rectifying the ways of teshuvah, so that on Rosh HaShanah we merit walking in these holy ways of teshuvah. Therefore in truth one must <span class="kt">travel to true Tzadikim for Rosh HaShanah</span> — for they are able to draw these ways of teshuvah, and primarily to sweeten the severity of the <em>din</em> of Rosh HaShanah and to draw <em>simchah</em> upon oneself then — as Admo"r z"l warned that <span class="sq">on Rosh HaShanah one must be in <em>simchah</em></span>. As it is written in Nechemyah regarding Rosh HaShanah <span class="vs">(Nechemyah 8)</span>: <span class="v">"Eat rich foods and drink sweet drinks, and do not be sad — for the joy of Hashem is your strength"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of the <span class="kt">Shofar</span>, which is to arouse kindnesses and sweeten <em>dinim</em>, as is known — to arouse Hashem's mercy that He look only at all the good points in Yisrael and not look at our evil at all, until His immense mercy enters our hearts, until joy is drawn upon us, through which the essential teshuvah rectification comes. This is: <span class="v">"Fortunate is the people who know the <em>teru'ah</em>… in Your Name they shall rejoice all day, and in Your righteousness they shall be exalted"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 89)</span>. For the sound of the Shofar is the aspect of the <span class="kt">ten kinds of melody</span>, which are the essential joy. Therefore the ten <em>Halleluyahs</em> — the ten kinds of melody — conclude with <span class="v">"Praise Him with crashing <em>teru'ah</em>"</span> etc. Therefore on Shabbos when Rosh HaShanah falls, we do not blow the Shofar — for the essential joy, which is the rectification mentioned above, is drawn through Shabbos. When Rosh HaShanah falls on Shabbos, there is no need to blow the Shofar to arouse the joy that is the essential teshuvah, for the entirety of joy is drawn from Shabbos, which is a day of joy and gladness in all the worlds, as written in the Zohar HaKadosh.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 47 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 47 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is what our Sages said <span class="vs">(Rosh HaShanah 29b)</span>: <span class="sq">"When Yom Tov of Rosh HaShanah falls on Shabbos — in the <span class="kt">Mikdash they would blow</span>, but not in the <em>medinah</em>. After the Beis HaMikdash was destroyed, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakai instituted that they blow wherever there is a <em>Beis Din</em>."</span> For in the Mikdash — the upper point — or wherever there is a <em>Beis Din</em> such as in Yavneh — meaning wherever there are true Tzadikim, the aspect of Moshe, the upper point — there they must blow on Rosh HaShanah even on Shabbos. For the great Tzadikim merit <span class="sq">"a day that is entirely Shabbos, entirely teshuvah"</span> — they constantly do teshuvah upon teshuvah, always returning in teshuvah upon their previous perception etc. Therefore they can blow even on Shabbos, for they are expert in the halachah well — even when they blow on Shabbos, they will not come to any damage through it. But now, when we have no Beis HaMikdash and no proper <em>Beis Din</em> established as in their days — for we do not know where the true Tzadikim are who are so great and so expert in this halachah of teshuvah — therefore it is forbidden to blow on Shabbos, lest one carry it four <em>amos</em> in the public domain, lest one leave the ways of the expertise mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
For in truth, the Shofar contains many aspects — it arouses and breaks the heart greatly: <span class="v">"Can a Shofar be sounded in a city and the people not tremble?"</span> <span class="vs">(Amos 3)</span> — the aspect of the broken heart that the <em>ba'al teshuvah</em> must have. But after the broken heart must come <em>simchah</em>, as is written in his [Rabeinu's] words, and this too is the aspect of the Shofar. On Shabbos only joy is needed — it is forbidden to arouse any broken heart at all. Therefore it is forbidden to blow on Shabbos. This is the aspect of "lest he carry it four <em>amos</em> in the public domain" — the place of the <em>Sitra Achra</em>, where sadness holds sway — for there are all the <em>melachos</em> and commerce and the anxieties of <em>parnasah</em>, the aspect of sadness: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat."</span> But in the <span class="kt">Mikdash and wherever there is a great <em>Beis Din</em></span> — the aspect of those expert in the halachah — they know how to blow even on Rosh HaShanah on Shabbos. For they know how to be very careful not to arouse any broken heart on Shabbos through the Shofar, and not to leave the boundary of Shabbos holiness. Rather, through their blowing they merit teshuvah upon teshuvah — which is also needed on Shabbos — therefore they would blow even on Shabbos.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 48 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 48 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Malchuyos</em>, <em>Zichronos</em>, and <em>Shofaros</em></span> — all of which are to arouse the kindnesses of Hashem and His wonders, that He will certainly complete everything according to His will. He will cause the kindnesses to overcome the <em>dinim</em>, arouse the good in Yisrael, and they will all return to Him — when His <em>malchus</em> will be revealed to the eyes of all. This is the aspect of <em>Malchuyos</em> — the verses from <em>Tanach</em> of the revelation of His <em>malchus</em> forever and ever. And they immediately mention: <span class="v">"He has not beheld iniquity in Yaakov, nor has He seen perversity in Yisrael"</span> <span class="vs">(Bamidbar 23)</span> — meaning, as above, that the Holy One Blessed Be He does not look upon the iniquity and blemishes of Yisrael, as Rashi explains there. And all the verses of <em>Zichronos</em> speak of how Hashem will remember His covenant forever and relent according to His abundant kindness. Therefore they begin with: <span class="v">"And G-d remembered Noach"</span> <span class="vs">(Beraishis 8)</span> — to show that even in the generation of the Flood, in the intensity of the <em>dinim</em>, He did not abandon His kindness but desired the world's continuance. How much more so now, when there have already been such great and awesome Tzadikim — the forefathers, Moshe Rabeinu, the seven shepherds, <em>Tannaim</em>, <em>Amoraim</em>, and Nevi'im — and in every generation great Tzadikim are found, and in every Jew are found many good points. Surely Hashem will remember His kindnesses in the merit of our forefathers — as the verses recited there state: <span class="v">"And I will remember My covenant with Yaakov"</span> etc., and <span class="v">"Is Ephraim My precious son?… I will surely have mercy on him"</span> etc., and <span class="v">"I remember for you the kindness of your youth"</span> etc.
</p>

<p class="p">
And likewise the verses of <em>Shofaros</em> — all to arouse the merit of <em>Matan Torah</em> etc. And they also speak of the ingathering of the exiles of Yisrael — which comes about through looking and finding merit in everyone. They also speak there of the ten <em>Halleluyahs</em>, the ten kinds of melody, made through the Tzadikim' extraction of good, as is written elsewhere. And they conclude: <span class="v">"And on the day of your joy and on your festivals… and you shall sound"</span> etc. — for the essential thing is to arouse the good in Yisrael until they merit joy, which is the essential aspect of the ways of teshuvah — the holiness of Shabbos, the root of teshuvah, as mentioned above.
</p>

<p class="p">
And this is: <span class="v">"He has not beheld iniquity in Yaakov"</span> — the aspect of the lower point, <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — as Rashi explains: the Holy One Blessed Be He does not look upon the sins and transgressions they commit etc. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — one must believe that even when a person falls, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, to wherever he falls, Hashem is still with him, His mercy is still upon him, waiting for him to return. Through receiving this expertise from the true Tzadik, one merits always returning to Hashem from wherever one is — the aspect of <em>baki b'nafik</em>. <span class="v">"Hashem his G-d is with him"</span> — the upper point. <span class="v">"And the <em>teru'ah</em> of a king is in him"</span> — the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em>, the aspect of the mitzvah of appointing a king, as is written there.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 49 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 49 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
It has been explained above that the essential rectification of teshuvah is to merit that <span class="kt">Moshe illuminate Yehoshua</span> — the unity of sun and moon — primarily through the joy of Shabbos drawn upon oneself. For the essential rectification of the blemish of the moon — the aspect of Yehoshua — is through the nullification of sadness. The blemish of the moon is <em>"me'oros"</em> [luminaries] spelled deficiently — and the Zohar HaKadosh explains, brought in his [Rabeinu's] words: <span class="sq">"<em>Me'oros</em> deficient — this is <em>Lilis</em>"</span> — from which comes all the wailing and sadness, from which comes all damage to the <em>Bris</em>, which is the essential <em>yetzer hara</em>. Therefore at the time of <span class="kt"><em>Kiddush Levanah</em></span> they mention the great joy of the luminaries: <span class="sq">"Joyous and glad to do the will of their Creator"</span> etc. And Yisrael customarily recite <em>Birkas HaLevanah</em> with great joy — as the Shulchan Aruch says, it is customary to make dances and circles at the time of <em>Kiddush Levanah</em>. This is the aspect of filling the blemish of the moon, which is through joy. And this is why they warned Yehoshua so many times: <span class="v">"Be strong and courageous"</span> — at the end of the Torah regarding Moshe's passing and at the beginning of the Book of Yehoshua — for the essential <em>hischazkus</em> [strengthening] is through joy: <span class="v">"For the joy of Hashem is your strength"</span> <span class="vs">(Nechemyah 8)</span>. Through this comes the essential unity and illumination from Moshe to Yehoshua, which is the essential rectification of the ways of teshuvah.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 50 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 50 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Lechem Mishneh</em></span> [the double bread] of Shabbos — the aspect of <em>Mishneh Torah</em>, the aspect of <span class="v">"double unto <em>tushiyah</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Iyov 11)</span>. All this is the aspect of <span class="kt">teshuvah upon teshuvah</span>. For the essential teshuvah upon teshuvah is merited by the true Tzadik who has already merited doing teshuvah in the ultimate completeness and purity, without any ulterior motive — a completeness beyond which there is none. Nevertheless, he does teshuvah again — teshuvah upon his teshuvah — upon his earlier perception etc., as is written there. This is the aspect of <span class="sq">"a day that is entirely Shabbos, entirely teshuvah"</span> — which they will merit in the World to Come. The great Tzadikim merit this in this world. In the World to Come: <span class="v">"no eye has seen"</span> etc. Only this is truly teshuvah upon teshuvah in completeness — the holiness of Shabbos, which is entirely teshuvah. For the rest of people, even if they exert themselves properly and do teshuvah upon their first teshuvah — that is, upon their sins — because it was not pure and without ulterior motive, and so again afterward — all this is not called teshuvah upon teshuvah in truth, but only by borrowed name. For as long as a person has not purified himself to the ultimate degree — until no ulterior motive or foreign thought arises in his mind — he is still not a complete <em>ba'al teshuvah</em>, since there remains some trace of contamination. Therefore all those teshuvos done each time because the previous teshuvah was not pure enough — all are called "teshuvah one." Only when through his many teshuvos he arrives at complete teshuvah in the ultimate purity — that is called the first teshuvah. And afterward, when he does teshuvah upon his earlier perception — that is the essential teshuvah upon teshuvah. And certainly even this great Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe who merits this, still has free choice in this — for in this world even the greatest Tzadik like Moshe Rabeinu has free choice. The essential free choice of the Tzadik who is the aspect of Moshe is in the aspect of <span class="sq">"Moshe added one day of his own understanding"</span> <span class="vs">(Shabbos 87)</span>, as is written elsewhere <span class="vs">(siman 190)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
This was the aspect of <span class="kt">Adam HaRishon's free choice</span> before the sin — he was utterly pure and the <em>yetzer hara</em> had not yet been placed within him, as our Sages said and as brought in Rashi's commentary <span class="vs">(Beraishis 2:25)</span>. If so, from where did he have free choice that he sinned with the Tree of Knowledge? Rather, all his choice was in this aspect — <span class="sq">"Moshe added one day of his own understanding."</span> He was completely pure, and Hashem warned him not to eat from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil — not to eat from where evil has a hold. Hashem wanted him never to enter the physical <em>yetzer hara</em> at all — only to retain the free choice of the great Tzadikim, the aspect of Moshe: to constantly advance from level to level, doing teshuvah upon one's previous perception. This is the holiness of Shabbos — a day holy and awesome, with no hold of evil, no <em>birur</em>. The entire <em>avodah</em> of Shabbos is to ascend from perception to perception — teshuvah upon teshuvah in truth — the <em>avodah</em> of the great Tzadikim. Therefore the stumbling of Adam HaRishon's sin came through <span class="kt">adding to the command</span>, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Midrash Shochar Tov 1)</span> — and through this they stumbled completely. It is certainly forbidden to add to Hashem's words: <span class="v">"You shall not add to it"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 13)</span>. Yet we find that Moshe Rabeinu added one day of his own understanding and Hashem agreed — and there are certainly many distinctions in this, for all the earlier sages added decrees and safeguards to most mitzvos, <em>shvus</em> for Shabbos etc. For us, it is absolutely forbidden to subtract or add. But the great Tzadikim have free choice in this — there are things they must understand from their own mind whether to add or not. There is great toil in this, as I understood from his [Rabeinu's] words when he spoke of this. This was the secret of Adam HaRishon's free choice; when he stumbled through his wife who added improperly, he stumbled greatly — for when one does not merit aligning one's mind with His will, one can stumble greatly. This is the aspect of <span class="sq">"Four entered the <em>Pardes</em>"</span> — only Rabbi Akiva entered in peace and exited in peace, but Ben Azai and Ben Zoma — one peered and was stricken, one peered and died — yet they remained great Tzadikim. But <em>Acher</em> fell completely and <span class="sq">"cut the saplings."</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
For this matter is the aspect of the expertise mentioned above — <em>baki b'ratzo baki b'shov</em>. The Tzadik must always ascend to a higher perception, yet it is forbidden for him to <span class="v">"break through to ascend to Hashem"</span> <span class="vs">(Shemos 19)</span> — to enter perceptions not fit for him, as explained in his [Rabeinu's] words (in the Torah <em>"Ki Merachamam"</em> siman 7, Likutay Tinyana, on <span class="sq">"A fence for wisdom is silence"</span> etc.). One who merits this expertise knows what to add according to His will and what not to add. Therefore only Rabbi Akiva entered in peace and exited in peace — <em>entered and exited</em> specifically, the aspect of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> — he was expert in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. But Ben Azai and Ben Zoma erred in this expertise and did not exit in peace, though they remained righteous. But <em>Acher</em> cut the saplings — he went entirely outside. This is the aspect of Adam HaRishon's sin — he was very elevated and stumbled in several aspects, as hinted in the Zohar HaKadosh <span class="vs">(Tikkun 69, 102)</span>: <span class="sq">"On that day he peered and died — for he gazed upon what he had no permission [to see]."</span> And our Sages said <span class="vs">(Sanhedrin 38b)</span> that he denied the <em>Ikkar</em> — only he immediately did teshuvah. All this was damage to the expertise, and all his rectification was through Shabbos — <span class="sq">"Shabbos protected him."</span> For Shabbos holiness is in this aspect of teshuvah upon teshuvah in truth — upon the earlier perception — the aspect of <span class="sq">"a day that is entirely Shabbos, entirely teshuvah."</span> But this is merited only by the great Tzadikim, the aspect of Moshe — only they know and perceive the secret of Shabbos holiness in completeness. From them we receive Shabbos holiness, in the aspect of <span class="sq">"Go and inform them."</span> Through this, we too can merit teshuvah upon teshuvah according to our level — that we constantly return to Hashem because the earlier teshuvah was not pure enough. Would that we merit even this for now!
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> of Shabbos — teshuvah upon teshuvah. The essential <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> derives from the <span class="kt"><em>Man</em></span> [manna] that fell on the sixth day as a double portion; on Shabbos it did not fall at all. The <em>Man</em> is the aspect of <em>da'as</em>, the aspect of Moshe, as Rabeinu z"l said <span class="vs">(siman 56)</span>. Yisrael merited through Moshe that on the sixth day a double portion fell for Shabbos. During the six weekdays of <em>berurim</em>, the <em>Man</em> fell daily according to that day's <em>birur</em> — the teshuvah of the common people, sorting good from evil, <em>kasher</em> from <em>pasul</em>, corresponding to the six orders of <em>Mishnah</em>. On the sixth day the <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> fell — the common people's teshuvah upon teshuvah, completing their teshuvah. On Shabbos the <em>Man</em> did not fall — then they eat the <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> of the sixth day, the secret of <em>Oneg Shabbos</em>. For then there is no <em>birur</em> and no toiling for sustenance — only eating the Shabbos meal, delighting in Hashem with the <em>Lechem Mishneh</em>, the teshuvah upon teshuvah of the great Tzadikim, the aspect of Moshe who is himself the <em>Man</em>, the <em>da'as</em>, whose entire teshuvah is upon his earlier perception. On Shabbos there is no <em>birur</em>, but the essential ascent of the worlds is on Shabbos, as brought in the <em>kavanos</em>. This is what Adam HaRishon needed — before the <em>yetzer hara</em> entered him, he needed no <em>birur</em> like ours, only to elevate the worlds. This is the essential Shabbos holiness merited by the great Tzadikim — teshuvah upon teshuvah in truth. Through their teshuvah, the worlds dependent on them ascend. Therefore the true Tzadikim merit the essential <em>Lechem Mishneh</em>, the <em>Mishneh Torah</em>. We too are obligated to break bread on <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> as a remembrance of the <em>Man</em> — for we draw teshuvah according to our level through the power of the great Tzadikim. For as explained above, through these Tzadikim — the essential Shabbos holiness that is entirely teshuvah of the World to Come — even the worst of the worst can merit teshuvah. Each time the Tzadikim do teshuvah upon their previous perception, they perceive His kindnesses more greatly, raise up the lowly further, and instill in them the expertise — that they merit <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — drawing themselves to Hashem from wherever they are, crying and praying always: <span class="v">"From the belly of She'ol I cried"</span> <span class="vs">(Yonah 2)</span>, <span class="v">"Waters flowed over my head; I said, I am cut off. I called Your Name, Hashem, from the lowest pit"</span> <span class="vs">(Eichah 3)</span> — until <span class="v">"He gazes and sees from Heaven"</span> and they merit complete teshuvah. All this is the Shabbos holiness each one receives from the great Tzadikim — the aspect of Moshe, <span class="sq">"Shabbos of all the days."</span>
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 51 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 51 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt">Yom Kippur</span>. Then everyone is obligated to fast, and beforehand on <em>Erev Yom Kippur</em> it is a mitzvah to eat. For eating connects the body with the soul — through eating, vitality is drawn to the body from the soul, which is the sustaining of the person. Through eating properly, the rectification mentioned above is accomplished — the <em>Aleph</em> etc., through which <em>Adam</em> [man] is formed: <em>Aleph-Dam</em> [A-blood]. The body is the lower point and the <em>neshamah</em> the upper point — body and soul are the aspect of heaven and earth, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Sanhedrin 91b)</span>: <span class="v">"He calls to the heavens above"</span> — this is the <em>neshamah</em>, <span class="v">"and to the earth"</span> — this is the body. Heaven and earth are the upper and lower points — <span class="v">"the heavens are My throne"</span> and <span class="v">"the earth is My footstool."</span> The vitality drawn through eating, which connects them, is the <em>Vav</em> within the <em>Aleph</em>. Therefore all the <em>kavanos</em> of eating pertain to this — the <em>Aleph</em> which is two <em>Yuds</em> and a <em>Vav</em> between them, and the <em>Vav</em> divides into two, making <em>Yud-Vav</em> above and <em>Yud-Vav</em> below, both equaling <em>lamed-beis</em> [32] — the thirty-two teeth that grind the food, corresponding to the thirty-two paths of <em>Chochmah</em>. The essential vitality flows to the heart, where all the seething of the bloods occurs. One must eat in the holiness of Yisrael so that the vitality flows from the <em>neshamah</em> to the body in holiness — vivifying the bloods of the heart in holiness, preventing the evil blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em> from gaining hold. When the bloods are pure and receive only holy vitality through eating, <em>Adam</em> is formed — <em>Aleph-Dam</em>. Adam HaRishon damaged this through eating from the Tree of Knowledge, and every person's sins derive from the damage to the bloods of the <em>chalal has'mali</em>. Therefore on <span class="kt">Yom Kippur</span>, the essential teshuvah, one must fast to rectify all sins that come from the blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em> — subdued through fasting. This is the aspect of the fasts of <em>ba'alei teshuvah</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore before Yom Kippur one must eat on <em>Erev Yom Kippur</em> — for when one wishes to return, one must be <span class="kt">expert in the halachah</span>, <em>baki b'ayeil baki b'nafik</em>. <span class="sq">"One who comes to purify — they help him and say: Wait!"</span> The eating of <em>Erev Yom Kippur</em>, which is a mitzvah, is the aspect of eating on Shabbos Kodesh — drawing the settling of mind, the aspect of <span class="sq">"Wait!"</span> — the expertise in halachah. Therefore on Yom Kippur when all Yisrael must return in complete teshuvah — one cannot snatch and fast immediately. First, on <em>Erev Yom Kippur</em>, one must eat, and this is a great mitzvah. For the mitzvah of eating on <em>Erev Yom Kippur</em> is the rectification of <em>baki b'nafik</em>, and the mitzvah of fasting and the <em>avodah</em> of Yom Kippur is the aspect of <em>ayeil</em>. Both are needed — one cannot return without the expertise in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 52 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 52 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore the <span class="kt">Kohen Gadol</span> would change on Yom Kippur <span class="kt">five times</span> — from outside to inside and from inside to outside, from golden garments to white garments and back. All this is the aspect mentioned above: rectifying <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> at its root. For even in the <em>avodah</em> of the Beis HaMikdash itself — which is entirely holy and is certainly <em>ayeil</em> relative to the entire world — even there, there is inside and outside. The Kohen Gadol's entering <em>Lifnai v'Lifnim</em> [the Holy of Holies] is the aspect of <em>ayeil</em> for him; then he exits to the <em>Heichal</em> and <em>Azarah</em> for the outer <em>avodah</em>. He alternated several times — all to rectify <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> at its supernal root. Through the awesome acts of his <em>avodah</em> inside the Holy of Holies and outside in the Beis HaMikdash, he rectified and drew the way of teshuvah into all the worlds, at all levels — that the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> be drawn upon everyone, which is the essential way of teshuvah. This is what we wrote above: we have no power to return in teshuvah except through the great Tzadikim who do teshuvah upon teshuvah constantly and are expert in the halachah of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> exceedingly well, according to their wondrous path — and they draw the expertise upon us.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore on Yom Kippur they begin the Torah reading with <span class="v">"After the death of the two sons of Aharon, when they drew near before Hashem and died"</span> <span class="vs">(Vayikra 16)</span>. For the entire damage of <span class="kt">Nadav and Avihu</span> — who were awesome Tzadikim — was in the aspect of the expertise: they did not rectify the aspect of <em>nafik</em>, the aspect of <em>shov</em>. They sought to draw close to Hashem in <em>devekus</em> beyond the measure — <span class="v">"lest they break through to ascend to Hashem"</span> <span class="vs">(Shemos 19)</span>. Therefore Hashem warned Aharon: <span class="v">"Let him not come at all times into the Holy"</span> — lest he die as his sons died through damaging the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. Rather, <span class="v">"With this shall Aharon come into the Holy"</span> — through the entire order of the Yom Kippur <em>avodah</em>. All this is read on Yom Kippur, for we cannot receive the way of teshuvah except through Tzadikim expert in this halachah — the <em>avodah</em> of Yom Kippur. From there the expertise is drawn upon us: the way of teshuvah to walk in all our days.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 53 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 53 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
After Yom Kippur we make <span class="kt">Sukkos</span>. The Zohar HaKadosh says that during all the days of Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur there was a great war between Yaakov and Eisav — Eisav coming to receive the blessings, and Yaakov taking them through wisdom. After Yom Kippur: <span class="v">"And Yaakov traveled to Sukkos"</span> — Yaakov, the aspect of <em>Klal Yisrael</em>, conceals himself within the holy <em>Sukkah</em>. On Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur, Yisrael fight the war of Amalek through the power of the true Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe — for then they exert themselves to return in teshuvah. The essential teshuvah is to cut off the seed of Amalek in all aspects — physically, to cut off his seed (the <span class="sq">"dominion of wickedness"</span> for which we pray much on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur), and to subdue the evil blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em>, the contamination of Amalek. Because this is a very long war, Hashem Himself must fight — only through the Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe, who draws the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, the entire <em>avodah</em> of the Kohen Gadol on Yom Kippur. Through all the prayers, supplications, and <em>selichos</em> of the Ten Days — especially Yom Kippur — the intention is not only to pray about the past, but primarily to beseech that Hashem help us henceforth with the way of teshuvah, that we not sin again: <span class="sq">"Place in our hearts [the will] to abandon the wicked path"</span>… <span class="sq">"May it be Your will that I not sin again"</span> etc. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"For on this day He shall atone for you, to purify you from all your sins"</span> <span class="vs">(Vayikra 16)</span> — the essential atonement is that through it we merit purification henceforth.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore after Yom Kippur we make Sukkos — the aspect of the <span class="kt">Clouds of Glory</span> that surrounded Yisrael in the wilderness, protecting them from the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Rosh HaShanah 3)</span> on the verse <span class="v">"The Canaanite king of Arad heard"</span> — this was Amalek, who heard that the Clouds of Glory departed upon Aharon's death. After Yom Kippur, each Jew must enter the holy <em>Sukkah</em>, the Clouds of Glory, to shelter from the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek: <span class="v">"For He will shelter me in His <em>Sukkah</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 27)</span>. The war of Amalek is very long — even after Yom Kippur one needs many stratagems to be saved from him, therefore we flee into the <em>Sukkah</em>. Especially when one goes outside the <em>shul</em> and <em>Beis Midrash</em> to attend to bodily needs and commerce — the aspect of <em>nafik</em>, where he ambushes greatly. By leaving the house for the <em>Sukkah</em>, the Clouds of Glory that protect from Amalek, one rectifies the expertise, primarily <em>baki b'nafik</em>. In the overall scheme of these sacred times: Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur are the aspect of <em>ayeil</em> (engaged only in prayer), while Sukkos is the aspect of <em>nafik</em> — the essential mitzvah of <em>Sukkah</em> being eating, drinking, and sleeping, the aspect of <em>nafik</em>.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 54 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 54 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of the <span class="kt">Four Species</span> held in the hands. The Zohar HaKadosh says <span class="vs">(Tikkunei Zohar 29)</span>: <span class="sq">"Who has conquered? He who holds the weapons of war in his hands."</span> At first glance this is perplexing — in all wars one can see who has conquered: whoever defeated his enemy. How is the question relevant? And the answer — that the victor is the one who <em>holds</em> the weapons — seems the opposite: holding the weapons indicates one has <em>not</em> yet won! One who has completely won no longer holds weapons. But this can be understood according to the above: the war of Amalek is an exceedingly long war. Amalek is <span class="sq">"the ancient scourge for Yisrael,"</span> and the essential battle is with the <em>yetzer</em>, the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek who always ambushes. The essential subjugation is through <em>hischazkus</em> [strengthening] — the expertise, primarily <em>baki b'nafik</em>, <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span> Whatever passes over a person all his days, he must be very strong not to fall. This path must be learned from the Tzadikim and those attached to them, the aspect of Moshe and Yehoshua.
</p>

<p class="p">
Thus, in this war that every person has with his <em>yetzer</em> — the war of Amalek — the <span class="kt">entire victory is when one is strong in his <em>da'as</em></span> through the Tzadik, holding himself and standing in the bonds of war in whatever comes. As long as a person does not despair and strengthens himself to begin each time anew, still engaged in this war whose essential weapon is prayer — he is called <span class="sq">"one who conquers the war."</span> For in truth <span class="v">"the war belongs to Hashem"</span> — a person alone cannot conquer him, as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Kiddushin 30b)</span>: <span class="sq">"Were it not for the Holy One Blessed Be He helping him…"</span> — and <span class="v">"War for Hashem with Amalek from generation to generation."</span> But a person is obligated to strengthen himself, arousing himself each time anew, never retreating from this war and never despairing.
</p>

<p class="p">
Now we understand the words of the Zohar: <span class="sq">"Who has conquered? He who holds the weapons of war in his hands."</span> In this war of Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur to cut off Amalek's seed physically and spiritually — certainly one cannot yet see who has conquered, especially in this long exile, with the Beis HaMikdash destroyed and each person enduring what he endures. Yet in the overall picture, Yisrael have certainly already conquered — since they hold the weapons in their hands: <span class="kt">the <em>Lulav</em> and its species</span>. Since Yisrael still engage in this war, holding weapons of war — the Four Species with which they pray and complete the <em>Hallel</em> — they have certainly conquered. For in this war, the essential victory is when one holds oneself and holds the weapons, as Admo"r z"l wrote on the verse <span class="v">"When its waves rise, You still them"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 89)</span>: as long as a person strengthens himself in prayer and crying out to Hashem, he is in the category of one who conquers the war. This is what the Four Species held in the hands on Sukkos signify.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 55 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 55 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
The <span class="kt">Four Species</span> are the aspect of the rectification mentioned above — meriting the aspect of <em>Adam</em>. In this they are engaged from Elul until Shemini Atzeres — drawing the way of teshuvah to merit inclusion in the aspect of <em>Adam</em>, rectifying Adam HaRishon's sin and the sins of all generations. As explained in the <em>kavanos</em>: the Four Species are the four letters of <em>Havayah</em> — the <em>Hadas</em> is the <em>Yud</em>; the <em>Arvei Nachal</em> [willows] the first <em>Hei</em>; the <em>Lulav</em> the <em>Vav</em>; and the <em>Esrog</em> the final <em>Hei</em>. All this is the aspect of <em>Adam</em>, which equals 45 — the Name <em>Havayah</em> with <em>Alefin</em>. The <em>Yud-Hei</em> are the three <em>Rishonos</em> — the upper point, the <em>Segol</em>. The <em>Vav</em> is the <em>Lulav</em> — the drawing from upper point to lower point. The final <em>Hei</em> is the <em>Esrog</em> — the lower point. The essential <em>avodah</em> is to draw all the beauty and <em>hiddur</em> to the <span class="kt"><em>Esrog</em></span> — <span class="v">"the fruit of a beautiful tree"</span> — the <em>Hei tata'ah</em>, the lower point.
</p>

<p class="p">
The <em>Yud</em>, the <em>Hadas</em>, has a good scent — the vitality of the <em>neshamah</em>, the upper point. With it are included the <em>Arvei Nachal</em>, the first <em>Hei</em>. This is a deep secret. At first glance it is puzzling: the <em>Midrashim</em> explain that <em>Arvei Nachal</em> hint at the <span class="kt">sinners of Yisrael</span> who have neither taste nor scent, who must be bound into the bundle. Why then should the <em>Arvei Nachal</em> hinting at sinners be placed at the level of the first <em>Hei</em>, <em>above</em> the <em>Vav</em> and final <em>Hei</em> (the <em>Lulav</em> and <em>Esrog</em>)? But this is the aspect explained above (section 17) regarding the three Shabbasos of Bein HaMetzarim: specifically at the time of descent and <em>churban</em> — when one falls to contaminated places — one must enliven oneself through <span class="sq">"Ayeh"</span>, the <em>ma'amar sasum</em>, through seeking etc. The sinners who have fallen to degraded places — in order to include them in the holy bundle — must be bound specifically <em>above</em>, in the first <em>Hei</em>, the three <em>Rishonos</em> above the <em>Binyan</em>, the <em>ma'amar sasum</em>. For specifically from there is their rectification.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore we bless only on the <span class="kt"><em>Lulav</em></span> — for the essential thing is the drawing from upper point to lower point, revealing the glory and splendor of His <em>malchus</em>: the <em>Esrog</em>, the lower point, the aspect of Yehoshua — through whom the essential war to cut off Amalek is waged. As Amalek is subdued through Yehoshua receiving power from Moshe, so His <em>malchus</em> is revealed and glorified. The essential <em>hiddur</em> is seen only in the aspect of Yehoshua: <span class="v">"the firstborn of his ox, splendor is his"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 33)</span>, <span class="v">"And you shall place of your majesty upon him"</span> <span class="vs">(Bamidbar 27)</span> — the moon, <span class="v">"beautiful as the moon"</span> <span class="vs">(Shir HaShirim 6)</span>. In the aspect of Moshe one cannot see his glory — he is the sun, which cannot be gazed upon now, the <em>Kisei d'miskasya</em>: <span class="v">"They were afraid to approach him… and he placed a veil on his face"</span> <span class="vs">(Shemos 34)</span>. We have no power to receive the glorious splendor of His <em>malchus</em> except through the aspect of Yehoshua, the moon receiving from the sun/Moshe. Therefore all the <em>hiddur</em> is in the <em>Esrog</em>. As is written <span class="vs">(siman 140)</span>: <span class="sq">"In the hand of every Adam He seals"</span> <span class="vs">(Iyov 37)</span> — in the Tzadik himself the world has no grasp, only through his people. His people and <em>talmidim</em> are the aspect of Yehoshua — through them the wondrous splendor of the Tzadik's holiness is revealed, through which His <em>malchus</em> and boundless greatness are revealed.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 56 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 56 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore on <span class="kt">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, the end of Sukkos, great rectifications are made with the <em>Aravah</em> itself. Through the <em>avodah</em> of all the days of Sukkos with the Four Species, binding the <em>Aravah</em> above, the Tzadikim accomplished that now great rectification can be made with the <em>Arvei Nachal</em> themselves. For the essential rectification is that the hidden <em>kavod</em> in the <em>ma'amar sasum</em> be revealed. This is the aspect of taking the <em>Aravah</em> alone on the seventh day of Sukkos, Hoshana Rabbah — for already the sinners of Yisrael have been rectified until His <em>kavod</em> is revealed even in them, from concealment to revelation.
</p>

<p class="p">
Now you will see wonders: our Sages said <span class="vs">(Sukkah 44)</span> that the taking and striking of the <em>Aravah</em> on this day is a <span class="kt"><em>minhag Nevi'im</em></span> [a custom of the Prophets] — <em>Nevi'im</em> specifically! For as explained above (section 37), all the Nevi'im are included in the aspect of Yehoshua, the lower point. Therefore the rectification of the <em>Aravah</em> — the sinners of Yisrael who have been brought back in teshuvah — is through the <em>Nevi'im</em> specifically, who are the aspect of Yehoshua, through whom the essential rectification of the way of teshuvah comes — drawing the essential rectification of finding Him everywhere: <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span>
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 57 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 57 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of the <span class="kt"><em>na'anu'im</em></span> [shakings of the <em>Lulav</em>]. In each direction one extends from opposite the heart and returns — the aspect of <em>ratzo v'shov</em>. One moves forth and brings back, raises up and brings down — the aspect of the expertise: <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven, there You are, and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — and likewise in every direction, as the verse concludes <span class="vs">(Tehillim 139)</span>: <span class="v">"If I take up the wings of dawn, if I dwell at the far end of the sea — even there Your hand will guide me"</span> etc. Whether raising the <em>Lulav</em> and its species or lowering them, and in every direction, there are movements forth and back — the <em>na'anu'im</em>. All this is what was written above (section 29) regarding <span class="sq">"two that are four"</span>: both in <em>ayeil</em> — <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven"</span> — there is <em>ratzo v'shov</em>, and in <em>nafik</em> there is <em>ratzo v'shov</em>, in many aspects. One must be very expert in all this — therefore they shake in many <em>na'anu'im</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore on Shabbos one does not take the <em>Lulav</em>, for the same reason as the Shofar — lest one carry it four <em>amos</em>, which is the damage to the expertise. For taking the <em>Lulav</em> too is to rectify the aspect of <em>Adam</em>, through the expertise whose essential source is Shabbos observance.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 58 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 58 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, when the holiness is sealed in the aspect of <span class="kt">seal within seal</span> [<em>chosam b'soch chosam</em>], as explained in the <em>kavanos</em> of the Arizal. <em>Chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em> is the aspect of teshuvah upon teshuvah. Therefore <em>chosam</em> [seal] in <em>gematria</em> equals three times <em>Ehyeh</em> in three <em>miluyim</em> [spellings], as written in the <em>kavanos</em>. For <em>Ehyeh</em> is the aspect of teshuvah — <span class="sq">"I am ready to become"</span> [<em>ana zamin l'meihevai</em>]. In this they are engaged all the days of Elul, and primarily from Rosh HaShanah to Yom Kippur — the Ten Days of Teshuvah — engaged in doing teshuvah and rectifying the ways of teshuvah for oneself and all who come into the world, that all strengthen themselves to walk in the ways of teshuvah always and not return to folly. One must know and believe that <em>Klal Yisrael</em> accomplish great and awesome rectifications in this every year — that the essence of all the rectifications and holy <em>kavanos</em> explained in the Zohar, the writings, and the holy books, is to draw rectifications and sweeten the <em>dinim</em>, to nullify the <em>yetzer hara</em> and <em>kelipos</em> that feed from the <em>dinim</em>, so that we merit walking in the ways of teshuvah always. Therefore Admo"r z"l explained in his awesome and wondrous way that all the <em>kavanos</em> of Elul explained in the writings of the Arizal are included in the aforementioned Torah, which revealed the way to teshuvah — the expertise in halachah.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore on Yom Kippur, when Yisrael complete the rectification of the way of teshuvah, the first seal is formed — three times <em>Ehyeh</em>, the aspect of teshuvah: sealing the holiness that we always walk in holiness and never turn back. But as explained, in the overall holiness of Tishrei, until Yom Kippur is the aspect of <em>ayeil</em>, and on Sukkos we rectify <em>nafik</em> (section 53). Therefore at the end of Sukkos — Hoshana Rabbah — they rectify <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em>, teshuvah upon teshuvah. For <em>baki b'ayeil</em> and <em>baki b'nafik</em>, <em>baki b'ratzo</em> and <em>baki b'shov</em> — <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven, there You are, and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — upper point and lower point, Moshe and Yehoshua, sun and moon: all are one aspect, all teshuvah upon teshuvah. One who receives this expertise from the true Tzadik constantly does teshuvah upon teshuvah at whatever level — both in ascent and in descent — strong in his <em>da'as</em> always to draw himself to the ways of teshuvah however things may be. Therefore on Sukkos, when <em>nafik</em> is rectified, at its conclusion — Hoshana Rabbah — the <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em> is completed: <span class="v">"Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm — for love is as strong as death"</span> <span class="vs">(Shir HaShirim 8)</span>. <em>Chosam</em> in <em>gematria</em> equals two times <em>Derech</em> [way] (with the <em>Vav</em> of conjunction) — for the essential seal rectifies the <span class="kt">ways of teshuvah</span>, which are the aspect of <em>Ehyeh</em> in all aspects — the <em>chosam</em> of three times <em>Ehyeh</em>, equaling two times <em>Derech</em>.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 59 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 59 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore on the <span class="kt">night of Hoshana Rabbah</span> they study the book of <em>Mishneh Torah</em> [Devarim]. For almost all of <em>Mishneh Torah</em> speaks about Eretz Yisrael — Yisrael were prepared to enter, but through the sin of the Golden Calf, the spies etc. there was a great delay of forty years, and Moshe Rabeinu himself had to pass away because of this. He hinted to them what would befall in the end of days — the Beis HaMikdash destroyed twice, the exile prolonged greatly, the concealment within concealment intensifying: <span class="v">"And I will surely hide My face"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 31)</span>. All the rectification to enter Eretz Yisrael is through the aspect of Yehoshua — <span class="v">"Yehoshua bin Nun… strengthen him… command Yehoshua and strengthen and encourage him"</span> — especially at the end of <em>Vayeilech</em> and <em>Ha'azinu</em>, urging Yehoshua's strengthening. All this hints at the aforementioned Torah: the essential rectification now is through the <em>Rebbe</em> of the generation, the aspect of Moshe, who greatly strengthens his <em>talmidim</em>, the aspect of Yehoshua, to walk in the ways of teshuvah always. Therefore on the night of Hoshana Rabbah — the seal upon the seal — they study <em>Mishneh Torah</em>, which speaks of Moshe's passing and Yehoshua's strengthening, through which the essential rectification of the ways of teshuvah comes.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 60 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 60 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore they also say the entire <span class="kt">Sefer Tehillim</span>, composed by David HaMelech. For the rectification of Moshe and Yehoshua — entering Eretz Yisrael — will ultimately be completed through David, the aspect of Mashiach. Yehoshua brought Yisrael into Eretz Yisrael, but the essential holiness of Eretz Yisrael was not completed until David found the place of the Beis HaMikdash — when <span class="v">"He gave you rest from all your enemies"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 12)</span> was fulfilled, and Rashi explains: <span class="sq">"This was only in the days of David."</span> All of David's rectification is the aspect of <em>Tehillim</em> — praises and songs to Hashem — the aspect of Shabbos holiness: <span class="v">"It is good to give thanks to Hashem"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 92)</span> in <em>Mizmor Shir L'Yom HaShabbos</em>. And within this, he cries out and supplicates and prays greatly to Hashem from wherever he is. Therefore Admo"r z"l wrote <span class="vs">(Likutay Tinyana, siman 73)</span> that <span class="sq">through Tehillim one merits teshuvah</span>. Now in the length of the exile, all our vitality is through the aspect of Moshe and Yehoshua — through these aspects we are strengthened to cry out to Hashem always, to gladden our souls in Shabbos holiness, to thank Hashem for the past and cry out for the future. This is the essential way of teshuvah. Through it we will all merit returning to Eretz Yisrael. Therefore on the night of Hoshana Rabbah they study <em>Mishneh Torah</em> — Moshe strengthening Yehoshua — and say <em>Tehillim</em> — songs, praises, prayers, and cries to Hashem — through which we will merit walking in the ways of teshuvah always, the seal upon the seal.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 61 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 61 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
<em>Chosam</em> [seal] has the same letters as <span class="kt"><em>Techum</em></span> [boundary] — the aspect of <em>Techum Shabbos</em>. For the essential rectification is through Shabbos, when the ways of teshuvah are rectified through guarding oneself from carrying outside four <em>amos</em> and from going outside the <em>techum</em>: <span class="v">"If you restrain your feet because of Shabbos"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayah 58)</span>, as explained above at length.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 62 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 62 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of the <span class="kt">cry of <em>"Hoshana"</em></span> that they cry much over the <em>Arvei Nachal</em>. Also all seven days of Sukkos, when they encircle the <em>tevah</em> [bimah] and cry "Hoshana" many times — all this is for the rectification of the <em>Arvei Nachal</em>. The circuits are a remembrance of the Mikdash, where they brought willow branches and encircled them. Especially on Hoshana Rabbah, when they cry "Hoshana" countless times — for the essential engagement throughout Sukkos, and especially on Hoshana Rabbah, is to rectify the aspect of <em>Arvei Nachal</em>, the sinners of Yisrael — to draw the ways of teshuvah so that they too return to Hashem and are strengthened to remain within Yisrael's holiness from wherever they are.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore they adopted the language <span class="kt"><em>"Hosha Na"</em></span> — to hint at a remembrance of the aspect of Yehoshua, who was called Yehoshua because <span class="sq">"<em>Yah</em> shall save you"</span> — as our Sages said <span class="vs">(Tanchuma, Shelach)</span>. Yehoshua was in great danger lest he be swept and ensnared in the counsel of the spies, who were all great men of Yisrael, and nevertheless were caught in the net of the <em>yetzer hara</em> and gravely damaged Moshe Rabeinu and Eretz Yisrael. Yehoshua was saved through having served Moshe Rabeinu greatly — he merited that Moshe blessed him and prayed for him: <span class="sq">"<em>Yah</em> shall save you."</span> For this he was called <em>Yehoshua</em>. Therefore they cry out much in the language of Yehoshua — <em>"Hosha Na"</em> and <em>"Hoshi'ah Na"</em> — to draw the aspect of Yehoshua, the rectification of the lower point: <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — which is the essential thing that needs to be rectified now at the end of Sukkos, on Hoshana Rabbah. For this one must cry out intensely from the depth of the heart: <em>"Hoshana"</em> and <em>"Hoshi'ah Na"</em> — all for the aspect of the <em>Arvei Nachal</em>. For even when they begin to return to Hashem they are in enormous danger, as is known — their entire strength and hope is the aspect of Yehoshua, whose teacher, the aspect of Moshe, prayed <span class="sq">"<em>Yah</em> shall save you."</span> This is the cry of <em>"Hoshana"</em> many times. Understand well from this the hints for yourself.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 63 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 63 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
The essential rectification of the <em>chosam</em> [seal] is through <span class="kt"><em>emes</em></span> [truth]. For the seal of the Holy One Blessed Be He is <em>emes</em>. The essential totality of all rectifications is <em>emes</em> — strengthening holy <em>emunah</em> in Hashem and in the Tzadikim — for the essential <em>emunah</em> is through <em>emes</em>, as we have written many times. This is the aspect of <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em>: <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven, there You are"</span> — that is seal one; <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — that is seal two. First one seals the holiness with seal one, which is <em>emes</em> — holding oneself on the path of truth always, not turning right or left, being strong to return in teshuvah always. This is the aspect of <em>ayeil</em> — though even in this there is <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, <em>ratzo</em> and <em>shov</em>, overall it is <em>ayeil</em>. Afterward on Sukkos they rectify <em>nafik</em> further through the power of the Tzadikim's service on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur. Then they must seal the holiness with seal two — whose essence is <em>emes</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
For sometimes a person can fall, <em>chas v'shalom</em>, through <span class="kt"><em>emes</em> itself</span> — knowing in his soul that he truly has damaged much, and even now he is as he is. Therefore the <em>ba'al davar</em> [adversary] entices him through his own <em>emes</em> to push him away completely — as we have heard these very words explicitly from many people who left their world entirely through this <em>emes</em>. But the true Tzadikim have already revealed to us that the <em>emes</em> to its true core is not so. This is the aspect of the expertise: every person who wishes to have mercy on himself and walk in the ways of teshuvah — the essential thing is to fulfill <span class="v">"If I ascend heaven, there You are, and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — even in <em>She'ol Tachtiyos</em> one is supported by Hashem, and there is no despair in the world whatsoever, as Admo"r z"l spoke much about. Certainly one must guard oneself from sin, from any damage whatsoever. But even so — even if one stumbled as one stumbled, even thousands and tens of thousands of times — at every moment <span class="v">"the kindnesses of Hashem have not ended"</span> — one can always draw oneself to Hashem from wherever one is. <span class="v">"Great is Hashem exceedingly, and His greatness is beyond investigation"</span> — there is a way in which everything will be turned to good, as explained in those holy conversations in his holy books, and even more than this he spoke with us mouth to mouth. This is the essential <em>emes</em> to its true core: seal two of the <em>nafik</em> aspect — <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em>, which guards the first <em>emes</em>: <span class="v">"Who guards <em>emes</em> forever"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 146)</span>. For <em>emes</em> itself needs great guarding — lest one err through one's own <em>emes</em> — and so in many aspects, as explained at length in our words.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 64 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 64 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Also, all the <span class="kt">dispute against the true Tzadikim</span> — whether the dispute among those who call themselves <em>chassidim</em> and proper people, and especially the dispute of the investigating sects who incline toward heresy and <em>apikorsus</em> — all of it comes through their <em>emes</em>. For <em>emes</em> has become <span class="sq">"flock upon flock"</span> — each one says the <em>emes</em> is with him. Therefore whoever looks sometimes into the books of the investigators will see that speech of <em>emes</em> never leaves their mouths; they cry only <em>emes</em>. Woe to them and to their counterfeit <em>emes</em>! (There is no need to elaborate here; much of this is explained in our words in several places.) Therefore <em>emes</em> itself needs guarding. This is the aspect of needing <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em> — for the essential seal is <em>emes</em>, and <em>emes</em> itself needs guarding. All this is through the power of the true Tzadikim and their <em>talmidim</em>, the aspect of Yehoshua, who draw the ways of teshuvah: <span class="v">"If I ascend… and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span> — the two expertises of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, which is completed on Hoshana Rabbah. Therefore the <em>chosam</em> upon <em>chosam</em> is made then. It is known in the <em>Kesavim</em>: <em>emes</em> in <em>gematria</em> equals <em>Ehyeh</em> times <em>Ehyeh</em>. Now the essential rectification is through the aspect of <em>Ehyeh</em>, the aspect of teshuvah — the <em>chosam</em> equaling three times <em>Ehyeh</em> in its <em>miluy</em>.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 65 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 65 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Also, <span class="kt"><em>Yehoshua</em></span> — with its five letters — equals in <em>gematria</em> <em>Mishneh</em> with the <em>kollel</em>: the aspect of <em>Mishneh Torah</em>. For the essential <em>Mishneh Torah</em> is to bring Torah fulfillment into Yisrael — as explained there, the abundance of warnings that Moshe gives to fulfill the Torah. The essential Torah fulfillment is through the aspect of Yehoshua, who received the Torah from Moshe, through whom the essential drawing of the ways of teshuvah occurs. Therefore they say <em>Mishneh Torah</em> on the night of Hoshana Rabbah.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore they stay awake on the <span class="kt">night of Hoshana Rabbah</span> — to rectify the measure of night, which is the aspect of the <em>levanah</em> [moon], the aspect of Yehoshua. The essential rectification is that Moshe illuminate Yehoshua — the sun to the moon.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 66 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 66 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
We return to the matter of <span class="kt">Shabbos</span>. It is explained in the <em>Midrashim</em> that the subduing of the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek — the totality of all the nations opposing Yisrael — is through the holiness of Shabbos. This is brought in the <em>Midrash</em> <span class="vs">(Pirkei d'Rabbi Eliezer 43)</span>, brought in <em>Sefer Mayim Yechezkel</em>, <em>Parshas Beshalach</em>: <span class="sq">"Yisrael said: Master of the Universe, You wrote in the Torah 'Remember the day of Shabbos'… and You wrote 'Remember what Amalek did to you' — how can these two remembrances both be fulfilled? The Holy One Blessed Be He said: They are not alike — this 'remember' is to sanctify, and this 'remember' is to kill and destroy him."</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the entire matter we have written above according to this Torah: the holiness of Shabbos is the holiness of the Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe, who does teshuvah upon teshuvah constantly, meriting in his lifetime "a day that is entirely Shabbos, entirely teshuvah." Through his great power, the aspect of Yehoshua fights the war of Amalek, subduing the blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em>. Therefore the <em>Midrash</em> connects these two remembrances — Shabbos and the destruction of Amalek — and asks how they can both be fulfilled, for they are truly opposites. At first glance it would seem proper to forget Amalek entirely — to expel his <em>kelipah</em> and contamination from the heart — and only remember Shabbos holiness, the Tzadik's holiness, the totality of Yisrael's holiness. The <em>Midrash</em> answers plainly: they are not alike — this <em>zachor</em> to sanctify, this <em>zachor</em> to kill and destroy. They are certainly two opposites, and therefore one must remember both — Shabbos holiness and, <em>l'havdil</em>, the deed of Amalek — in order to destroy and uproot the memory of Amalek through the remembrance of Shabbos holiness at every time. The remembrance of Amalek's deed is only so that we may uproot it — one must remember that Amalek constantly ambushes. Therefore one must strengthen oneself all the more in remembering Shabbos holiness — the holiness of the Tzadik who does teshuvah always — through whose power we too always have hope. Therefore precisely <em>because</em> they are opposites, one must remember them both well, to uproot the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek through the remembrance of Shabbos holiness — the Tzadik's holiness, through whose power the aspect of Yehoshua fights the war of Amalek.
</p>

<p class="p">
Also, it is explained in the <em>Gemara</em> <span class="vs">(Shabbos 118b)</span>: <span class="sq">"Had Yisrael kept the first Shabbos, no nation or tongue would have ruled over them, as it says: 'And it was on the seventh day, some of the people went out to gather' — and it is written after it: 'And Amalek came.'"</span>
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 67 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 67 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is what is written in <em>Tanna d'Vei Eliyahu</em> chapter 26 part 1, brought in <em>Midrash Rabbah Parshas Ki Sissa</em>: <span class="sq">"'And you shall guard the Shabbos for it is holy to you' — there are three holinesses: the holiness of the Name of the Holy One Blessed Be He, the holiness of Shabbos, and the holiness of Yisrael… from here they said: the teeth of those who devour Yisrael will in the future, in the days of Mashiach, be twenty-four <em>amos</em>"</span> — see there.
</p>

<p class="p">
All these <span class="kt">three holinesses</span> are included together through the true Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe, who draws and makes known Shabbos holiness. Through Shabbos holiness, the holiness of the souls of Yisrael ascends and is included in their root, in His holiness — this is the aspect of <em>Shabbos</em> = <em>Shin-Bas</em>: the <span class="v">"king's daughter within"</span> — the souls of Yisrael — ascends above. This is the <em>Shin</em>, the totality of the three holinesses. Therefore the Tzadik, the aspect of Moshe, is in the aspect of the <em>segol</em> — the totality of the three points. Therefore the <em>Midrash</em> connects to this the <span class="kt">downfall of the wicked</span> in the future, when their teeth will be many <em>amos</em> to their detriment: <span class="v">"the teeth of the wicked You have broken"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 3)</span> — for these depend upon each other. The downfall of the wicked, whose totality is Amalek, is through Shabbos holiness. Conversely, to the degree that each person overcomes all six weekdays to subdue the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek through the Tzadikim's power — Moshe and Yehoshua — so he merits drawing Shabbos holiness. Therefore the <em>Midrash</em> mentioned their downfall through the breaking of their teeth specifically — which at first glance is a great wonder, as all the commentators were puzzled. But according to our words there is a wonderful connection in this <em>Midrash</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
For the essential matter is Shabbos holiness, when <em>Kneses Yisrael</em> unites with her Beloved — <span class="v">"Come, my Beloved, to greet the bride"</span> — and then all three points are included together, completing the aspect of <em>Adam</em> to sit on the Throne. The essential Shabbos holiness is the <span class="kt">eating</span> <span class="vs">(as in <em>siman</em> 277 and 57, Likutay I)</span> — Shabbos eating is exceedingly precious, entirely Godliness, entirely holy. Through eating, the soul joins with the body and enlivens the blood of the heart — as in <em>os</em> 51 regarding <em>Erev</em> Yom Kippur eating. Therefore the <span class="kt">thirty-two teeth</span> correspond to the thirty-two paths of wisdom — the essential eating is through them, sustaining the heart's vitality. All damages stem from eating of the Tree of Knowledge — damaging the aspect of <em>lamed-beis</em> [32], strengthening the blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em>: the damage to the thirty-two paths, the damage to the thirty-two teeth. The essential rectification is through Shabbos. Therefore the rectification is through Shabbos eating specifically — the three meals corresponding to the three points (<em>os</em> 4). Through Shabbos eating, the thirty-two paths of wisdom are drawn, the thirty-two teeth chew Shabbos food in great holiness, and the blood of the heart is rectified — nullifying the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek, the totality of the wicked.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore the downfall of the wicked through their teeth is excellently juxtaposed with the greatness of Shabbos. All who stand against Yisrael to devour them — <span class="v">"they hissed and gnashed their teeth"</span> <span class="vs">(Eichah 2)</span> — their downfall will be through Shabbos holiness, the Tzadik's holiness, when the rectification is through the thirty-two teeth — the instruments of eating — through which the blood of the heart is refined. All who oppose this — the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek — their teeth will grow long to their detriment, through Shabbos holiness whose essential holiness is eating through the teeth. For the thirty-two teeth are the <em>aleph</em> of <em>Adam</em>, through which the aspect of <em>Adam</em> is made — as in <em>os</em> 51 regarding <em>Erev</em> Yom Kippur.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 68 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 68 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Kiddush</em> over wine on Shabbos</span>, derived from <span class="vs">(Shemos 20)</span>: <span class="v">"Remember the day of Shabbos to sanctify it"</span> — <span class="sq">"remember it over wine"</span> <span class="vs">(Pesachim 106)</span>. The essential <em>Kiddush</em> is to draw Shabbos holiness upon oneself, to sanctify all the sparks and souls refined during the weekdays that ascend at Shabbos's onset. Now we draw holiness upon them through <em>Kiddush</em>. Therefore we say <em>Vayechulu</em> over the cup, beginning <span class="v">"The sixth day, and the heavens were completed…"</span> — the entire passage speaks of how on the seventh day, Shabbos, the work was completed through the cessation and rest drawn upon all creations — "Shabbos came, rest came" — the completion and purpose of all His work. As Rashi explained: <span class="sq">"What was the world lacking? Rest…"</span> All this we mention at the start of <em>Kiddush</em> over wine — for the entire <em>Kiddush</em> is to draw Shabbos-rest's holiness upon all weekday labors, which are <em>berurim</em> that ascend on the sixth day toward evening at Shabbos's onset, to be included in their purpose — Shabbos holiness, the purpose of heaven and earth.
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore we sanctify over <span class="kt">wine</span> specifically. Refining the holy sparks from weekday contamination to sanctify them in Shabbos holiness — this is cutting off Amalek's seed through the lower point, the aspect of Yehoshua, subduing the blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em>. Therefore we sanctify over wine specifically — wine is the strengthening of the blood: <span class="sq">"Do not look upon wine when it reddens — its end is blood"</span> <span class="vs">(Sanhedrin 70 on Mishlei 23)</span>. One must be very careful about wine, especially on weekdays. Yet in holiness there are times when it is a mitzvah to drink wine — this is <em>Kiddush</em> over wine specifically. At Shabbos's onset, the blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em> is subdued — then the wine is in holiness: <span class="sq">"he merited — it gladdens him"</span> — <span class="v">"wine gladdens the heart of man."</span> Through the wine over which we sanctify, holiness is drawn upon all ascending sparks through the rectification of the lower point. For their essential ascent is through <em>simchah</em> (<em>os</em> 45).
</p>

<p class="p">
Amalek — the muddying of the blood — is the aspect of <span class="kt">sadness</span>, from which the strengthening of desires whose totality is the desire of <em>mishgal</em>: <span class="v">"that he chanced upon you on the way"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 25)</span>. On weekdays it is hard to subdue him through wine — most do not merit the gladdening wine, and then <span class="sq">"he did not merit — it devastates him."</span> But at Shabbos's onset one must draw Shabbos joy through the cup of wine over which one sanctifies — then it is the gladdening wine. Through this, holiness is drawn upon the blood — the souls and sparks ascending — and through the blood of the heart one burns for Hashem in <span class="v">"flames of the fire of <em>Yah</em>"</span> — Shabbos holiness is drawn upon all the sparks, their entire purpose. This is <span class="sq">"song is said only over wine"</span> <span class="vs">(Berachos 35, Arachin 11)</span> — their essential ascent is through song and praise to Hashem: the ten kinds of melody, <span class="v">"A psalm, a song for the Shabbos day"</span> — the ultimate delight of the World to Come, "a day entirely Shabbos," when they will merit the preserved wine and song will awaken in the world — as in the holy Zohar <span class="vs">(Tikkun 10, 24b)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
The essential ultimate rectification of all weekday labors is through the holiness of the <span class="kt">Beis HaMikdash</span> — the upper point — upon all thirty-nine <em>melachos</em> (derived from the <em>Mishkan</em>). The <em>meraglim</em> damaged the Beis HaMikdash by slandering Eretz Yisrael, when its destruction was decreed: <span class="sq">"You wept…"</span> <span class="vs">(Ta'anis 29)</span>. All their damage was through the <span class="kt">grapes</span> — the damage of wine, through which Adam HaRishon sinned: <span class="sq">"She squeezed grapes and gave him"</span> <span class="vs">(Bereishis Rabbah 19)</span>. Noach too stumbled upon leaving the <em>teivah</em>. Most spies took the cluster of grapes: <span class="v">"and the days were the days of the first-ripening of grapes"</span> <span class="vs">(Bamidbar 13)</span> — all their sin's stumbling was through grapes from which wine is made, through which their minds went astray saying it was impossible to conquer Eretz Yisrael. Their essential error was through the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek — they had great <em>da'as</em> and saw Amalek was exceedingly powerful, but did not overcome to nullify their understanding before Moshe Rabeinu who had the power to subdue it. They frightened Yisrael through Amalek specifically: <span class="v">"Amalek dwells in the <em>Negev</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Bamidbar Rabbah 16)</span>. They stumbled through the grapes specifically — for there the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek grips more: <span class="v">"their grapes are grapes of poison, bitter clusters"</span> — the strengthening of the blood.
</p>

<p class="p">
Yehoshua was saved from the spies' counsel through Moshe's power — he subdues and cuts off the <em>kelipah</em> of Amalek, so he was not ensnared by their grapes. On the contrary, he merited bringing all Yisrael into Eretz Yisrael. Therefore at <em>Kiddush</em>, when the lower point — Yehoshua — shines, we sanctify over wine specifically, for then the wine is in holiness. Yehoshua cut off the evil blood of the <em>chalal has'mali</em> — then wine is the gladdening wine: <span class="sq">"song is said only over wine."</span> Therefore on <span class="kt">Purim</span>, the essential cutting off of Amalek's seed, it is a great mitzvah to become intoxicated — Amalek is subdued through wine specifically, meriting <em>simchah</em>, for his essential subduing is through <em>simchah</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the <span class="kt">seventy words of <em>Kiddush</em></span> — to subdue the seventy nations whose totality is Amalek. The <em>ayin</em> [70] of <em>Amalek</em> is subdued through the wine of holiness in <em>Kiddush</em>, which equals seventy.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is what our Sages expounded: <span class="v">"Remember the day of Shabbos to sanctify it"</span> — <span class="sq">"remember it over wine"</span> (<em>Kiddush</em>), and also: <span class="sq">"remember it from the first day of the week."</span> These are one and the same: through <em>Kiddush</em>, holiness is drawn upon all weekday deeds and labors. "Remember it from Sunday" — one must remember Shabbos throughout all six weekdays to draw Shabbos holiness upon them, whose essential completion is through <em>Kiddush</em> over wine.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 69 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 69 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Our words are founded upon what Admo"r z"l wrote in the Torah above, <em>os</em> 4: <span class="sq">"When a person wants to walk in the ways of teshuvah, he must be expert in <em>halachah</em> and have two expertises — expert in <em>ratzo</em>, expert in <em>shov</em>, as it says: 'Praiseworthy is he who enters and exits.' This is 'If I ascend heaven, there You are' — <em>ayeil</em>, expert in <em>ratzo</em>; 'and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You' — <em>nafik</em>, expert in <em>shov</em>."</span> See there at the end, the secret of the <em>Kavanos</em> of Elul, where the plain meaning is explained: whoever wants to return must gird his loins and strengthen himself in Hashem's ways always, in whatever passes over him. See our words in many places where we spoke of this in many formulations, with awesome <em>eitzos</em> to strengthen oneself — the essential thing through the power of the true Tzadik.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is the <span class="kt">summary of our words in this entire <em>halachah</em></span>: to strengthen ourselves through meriting to receive Shabbos, through which we always remind ourselves through the true Tzadik's power — the aspect of Shabbos. These depend upon each other: the more one believes in the Tzadik and draws close, the more one merits drawing Shabbos holiness; through keeping Shabbos with <em>simchah</em>, one merits drawing close to the Tzadik — and through this one merits walking in the ways of teshuvah, drawing the expertise to hold oneself in whatever passes, however it may be. The essential means is through <span class="kt">Rosh HaShanah</span>, when they gather together — for the ways of teshuvah are drawn through Elul, Rosh HaShanah, and Yom Kippur. All Yisrael's service during these days is to draw these teshuvah pathways so that Yisrael merit teshuvah, through which the redemption from this exceedingly long exile will be brought near. Its end cannot be fathomed — <span class="v">"the end of wonders"</span> — but it is explained in the <em>Gemara</em> and all <em>Midrashim</em> that the essential matter depends on teshuvah. Therefore the true Tzadikim labor only to draw teshuvah pathways into the world, so that even the very far and fallen will be strengthened in teshuvah, through which everything is rectified. For now Hashem asks only a little arousal: <span class="sq">"Let them open in teshuvah like the eye of a needle, and I will open great gates."</span> But even this infinitesimal bit is exceedingly hard to begin without the great Tzadikim who draw the teshuvah pathways to strengthen all who wish to return, wherever they may be.
</p>

<p class="p">
All this is hinted in the <span class="kt">Zohar Chadash regarding Noach's <em>teivah</em></span> <span class="vs">(Parshas Noach, 24-25, Slavuta edition)</span>: Noach did not beseech for his generation. After leaving the <em>teivah</em> and seeing the whole world destroyed, he began weeping. The Holy One Blessed Be He answered: <span class="sq">"Foolish shepherd! Now you say this and not before?"</span> See there: the greatness of Moshe Rabeinu who gave his life for Yisrael; how Noach did not beseech because he did not even think he himself would be saved; how much the Holy One Blessed Be He wants people to beseech for Yisrael, even if they are as they are. See what is written on <span class="v">"And he sent the raven"</span> — this is Dovid, who called constantly like a raven; and on <span class="v">"And he sent the dove"</span> until <span class="v">"and she did not return to him again."</span> All of Noach's damage and its rectification is about the expertise above — he was not expert in this <em>halachah</em> of the ways of teshuvah like Moshe and the great Tzadikim who draw teshuvah pathways with wondrous expertise. Noach was a <em>Tzadik tamim</em>, but did not grasp that it was possible to go out, to lower himself, to look upon such wicked people, to engage with them, to find merit in them, to pray for them, to arouse them through some good point — all of which Moshe and the great Tzadikim after him engaged in with tremendous effort. All this is included in the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>. Therefore Noach thought: "If only I could save myself" — he did not grasp how far His mercies reach.
</p>

<p class="p">
He was forced to enter the <em>teivah</em> — built with lofty wisdom and holiness, with deep <em>kavanos</em> in its dimensions. It hints at teshuvah on Yom Kippur: <span class="sq">"Noach's <em>teivah</em> — this is Yom Kippur"</span> <span class="vs">(Zohar, Tikkun 21, 54b)</span>; <span class="sq">"And the <em>teivah</em> rested in the seventh month — this is Tishrei."</span> Also: Noach's <em>teivah</em> is the words of prayers and supplications that Yisrael pray. All of Noach's entering was <em>ayeil</em> — because he could not save himself with the world, not being expert, especially in <em>nafik</em>. Hashem commanded him to enter — <em>ayeil</em>. And just as he needed the command to enter, he needed the command to exit: <span class="v">"Go out from the <em>teivah</em>"</span> — for his leaving was the aspect of <em>nafik</em>. It is written <em>hotzei</em> but read <em>hayatzei</em>; Rashi: <span class="sq">"Tell them to leave; if they don't want to, bring them out against their will."</span> They did not want to leave willingly — hinting at everything we have written: whoever wants to return would prefer sitting always in the <em>beis midrash</em>. But Hashem wants the world's continuity — a person must frequently leave for bodily needs and livelihood, all of which is <em>nafik</em>. This is very hard — one knows the danger of going outside — but it cannot be otherwise. Hashem wants one to serve Him in this world specifically — which is entirely <em>nafik</em> relative to the upper worlds — and therefore even here, service must be in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is <span class="v">"and he sent the raven"</span> — the Zohar Chadash explains: Dovid, who called constantly like a raven. Hashem sent him from his kingdom; <span class="v">"And it went out, going and returning"</span> — <span class="v">"Dovid went up the ascent of olives, going and weeping, his head covered"</span> <span class="vs">(Shmuel II 15)</span> — he went out and returned in teshuvah, confessing and seeking mercy, until the waters dried. The dove was sent three times, hinting at the exiles until this final one: <span class="v">"she did not return to him again"</span> — this is why this exile is so long. But nevertheless He is prepared and waiting for when she will return. See all this well.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 70 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 70 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore the entire rectification was through the <span class="kt">rainbow of the covenant</span> that Hashem promised <span class="vs">(Bereishis 9)</span>: <span class="v">"My bow I have placed in the cloud."</span> The rainbow of the covenant is the aspect of Rosh HaShanah when the <em>shofar</em> is blown — <em>teki'ah</em>, <em>shevarim</em>, <em>teru'ah</em> — whose sign is <em>kash"t</em> [=<em>keshes</em>, rainbow], as brought in his words z"l. This is the power of the great true Tzadik — the rainbow of the covenant, as in the holy Zohar <span class="vs">(Vayikra 15, Pinchas 215)</span>. This is Shabbos holiness — the three meals are the three <em>shofar</em> sounds, the three points. He was promised that even within the cloud and very great darkness — <span class="v">"when I bring clouds upon the earth"</span> — even then <span class="v">"the rainbow will be seen in the cloud."</span> Through the Tzadik — the rainbow of the covenant — Shabbos holiness is drawn through the Rosh HaShanah gathering, when the holy sounds — sign <em>kash"t</em> — are blown, sustaining the gathering of Yisrael: <span class="v">"On that day a great <em>shofar</em> will be blown, and the lost in Ashur and the cast-away will come"</span> <span class="vs">(Yeshayah 27)</span>. Through all this, the teshuvah pathways are drawn — the expertise of <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, to hold oneself even in <em>She'ol Tachtiyos</em>: <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span> Through this, Yisrael will fulfill His will — to return at least <span class="sq">"like the eye of a needle."</span> As the Zohar Chadash states: <span class="sq">"If <em>Kneses Yisrael</em> opens teshuvah like the eye of a needle, I will open great gates."</span> But even this minimal teshuvah is exceedingly hard because the adversary fells one each time — rectifiable only through the expertise drawn by the Tzadik, the rainbow of the covenant. Therefore Hashem promised Noach not to worry upon leaving the <em>teivah</em> — <em>nafik</em> — for Noach saw the great provocation of the <em>yetzer hara</em>. But Hashem promised through the rainbow of the covenant — the true Tzadik encompassing all rectifications, drawing teshuvah pathways — the expertise through which everything is rectified until the complete redemption comes speedily in our days, <em>amen</em>.
</p>

<!-- ══════════ Section 71 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 71 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
Therefore we mention in <span class="kt">Birchas Zichronos</span> (<em>Mussaf</em> of Rosh HaShanah) at its beginning the matter of Noach: <span class="v">"And also Noach You remembered with love when You brought the Flood waters… therefore his remembrance came before You…"</span> We remind Him of His immense kindness and mercy — how He desires the world's continuity. Even in Noach's days, when such darkness was in the world, He remembered Noach, brought him from the <em>teivah</em>, and promised to sustain the world through the rainbow of the covenant — Rosh HaShanah's holiness. Now that we have already merited the <em>Avos</em> — as we mention their merit afterward: <span class="v">"And I will remember My covenant with Yitzchak"</span> <span class="vs">(Vayikra 26)</span> — and Moshe Rabeinu gave us the Torah, and such Tzadikim revealed the holiness of Rosh HaShanah and the greatness of the gathering — now it is fitting that He remember His covenant in their merit and draw upon us the teshuvah pathways, that we merit returning to Him and He to us, until the redemption is brought near speedily (see <em>os</em> 48).
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 72 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 72 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is the aspect of <span class="kt"><em>Eiruvei Techumin</em></span>: where one places the Shabbos meal, from there the <em>Techum Shabbos</em> begins. Shabbos is the aspect of teshuvah, whose essence is the two expertises — expert in <em>ayeil</em>, expert in <em>nafik</em> — the <em>Techum Shabbos</em> of two thousand <em>amos</em>, the two <em>alephim</em>. Through teshuvah an <em>aleph</em> is made. The essential expertise — the teshuvah pathways — is to be extremely careful not to go outside the <em>Techum Shabbos</em>, outside holiness's boundary. The totality of holiness is Shabbos holiness, and this expertise means: in whatever passes over him, one guards oneself not to fall completely, not to go outside the <em>techum</em>. Every person inevitably endures much — especially one who already sinned; when wanting to return, the ocean depths rage — foul thoughts, as understood from the Arizal's writings and our Sages' words. This is essential teshuvah: overcoming so as not to fall completely. It is impossible to detail for each individual — the variations are immense even within one person. But all is included in his holy, awesome words where he called this "expert" — one must be very expert: expert in <em>ratzo</em> and <em>ayeil</em> according to one's level, expert in <em>shov</em> and <em>nafik</em> according to one's level — to fulfill <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You"</span>: even in <em>She'ol Tachtiyos</em>, never despair. As explained in those conversations and deep <em>eitzos</em> in our books — fortunate is he who grasps them.
</p>

<p class="p">
All this is <em>Techum Shabbos</em>. The essential Shabbos holiness is Shabbos eating — entirely holy — through which teshuvah pathways are drawn. Therefore where one places the Shabbos meal-measure, from there the <em>techum</em> begins — one acquired <em>shvisah</em> there. The essential acquisition of Shabbos rest is through the Shabbos meal. As in <em>siman</em> 277, Likutay I, <span class="sq">"<em>Tzadik kaTamar yifrach</em>"</span>: the holy path is conquered and broadened through Shabbos eating — <span class="sq">"check in the teeth and you will find in the feet"</span> <span class="vs">(Shabbos 152)</span>. The essential holy path the Tzadik makes — the teshuvah pathways — draws <em>eitzos</em> and <em>hischazekus</em> so that all return to Hashem, even the very weak who desire to return — all will merit through the teshuvah pathways drawn into the world, the aspect of Shabbos, essentially through Shabbos eating.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 73 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 73 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
The essential <span class="kt"><em>Techum Shabbos</em></span> is derived from the <em>Machaneh Yisrael</em>, which was twelve <em>mil</em>. Therefore <em>mid'Oraisa</em> the <em>techum</em> is twelve <em>mil</em>. But our Sages enacted only two thousand <em>amos</em>, seeing the great strengthening of the <em>kelipos</em> in exile. These two thousand <em>amos</em> are the measure between the <em>Aron</em> and the Camp: <span class="v">"But there shall be a distance between you and it of about two thousand <em>amos</em>"</span> <span class="vs">(Yehoshua 3)</span>. Rashi: <span class="sq">"Like the measure of <em>Techum Shabbos</em>, so they could go to pray before it."</span> The essential war is the war of Amalek — against the <span class="kt">weak ones</span> whom the cloud expelled. Yehoshua fought with Moshe's power so Amalek would not fell them completely: <span class="v">"and he tailed you — all the weakened ones behind you"</span> <span class="vs">(Devarim 25)</span>. Rather, to return them in teshuvah, bringing them within holiness's boundary — the <em>Machaneh Yisrael</em>, where the <em>Ohel Mo'ed</em> and <em>Aron</em> stood, from which holiness is drawn. The more distant one is, the more holiness and expertise must be drawn from a higher, holier place. Therefore the <em>techum</em>'s measure is from the <em>Machaneh Yisrael</em> — the essential <em>techum</em> is that each person remains in the Camp and Amalek cannot cast him out. One constantly strengthens oneself to hold onto the true Tzadikim and their <em>talmidim</em> — Moshe and Yehoshua — who fight Amalek in every generation to return and build the Beis HaMikdash and bring Yisrael back to Eretz Yisrael.
</p>


<!-- ══════════ Section 74 ══════════ -->
<div class="sn"><span>— 74 —</span></div>

<p class="p">
This is also <span class="kt"><em>Eiruvei Chatzeiros</em></span>: the essential <em>eiruv</em> rectification is through the Shabbos meal. Through sharing in the Shabbos meal, carrying in and out between domains becomes permitted. Without an <em>eiruv</em>, it is forbidden even between two <em>reshus hayachid</em> domains. The general prohibition of the thirty-nine <em>melachos</em> is the aspect of carrying from domain to domain — <em>berurim</em> (<em>os</em> 30). Our Sages were stringent that even from one domain to another is forbidden — all for the expertise's rectification. Just as one must be careful in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> not to leave one's boundary nor rush beyond it, one must not rush into one's fellow's boundary, in <em>ayeil</em> or <em>nafik</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
In <em>nafik</em>, certainly <em>chalilah</em> that one look at one's fellow to do as his deeds — one can lose his world in an instant. Dovid HaMelech warned <span class="vs">(Tehillim 1)</span>: <span class="v">"Happy is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the wicked… nor sat in the company of scoffers."</span> It is known how much the <span class="kt">scoffers of the generation</span> bring a person down — especially those calling themselves proper and pious. Hashem should save Yisrael from them. But even in <em>ayeil</em>, one must be very expert not to look at one's fellow who appears righteous compared to oneself, lest one fall — though <span class="sq">"envy of scholars increases wisdom"</span> <span class="vs">(Bava Basra 21)</span>, there is the aspect of <span class="v">"the righteous walk in them and transgressors stumble in them"</span> <span class="vs">(Hoshei'a 14)</span>. Many fall through jealousy of those who seem more righteous, falling completely in their mind. One must not be a fool but be expert: one's jealousy of better fellows should be for good, to draw closer to Hashem — as hinted in the story of the <em>Chacham</em> and the <em>Tam</em>, where the <em>Tam</em> answered: <span class="sq">"That is his work and this is my work."</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
Therefore our Sages enacted that even between domains, carrying on Shabbos is forbidden — so as not to damage the expertise. The rectification is through the Shabbos meal — the <em>eiruv</em>. Through the Shabbos meal the expertise is rectified, then carrying from house to house within the courtyard becomes permitted — the connection between domains will be only for good: they will join in love and peace, each learning from the other for good, drawing closer to Hashem. All this is the expertise in <em>halachah</em>, rectified through the Shabbos meal — the <em>eiruv</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is <span class="v">"And Hashem said to Avram: <em>Lech Lecha</em> — from your land, your birthplace, your father's house, to the land…"</span> and <span class="v">"I will make you a great nation"</span> <span class="vs">(Bereishis 12)</span>. As explained, the three <em>mitzvos</em> upon entering Eretz Yisrael — cutting off Amalek's seed, appointing a king, building the Beis HaBechirah — are the three points, the aspect of teshuvah. The essential teshuvah is <span class="sq"><em>"zarka</em> — cast to the place from where it was taken"</span> — one must leave all the places to which one strayed and return to the source from which one's <em>neshamah</em> was hewn. Every Jew's <em>neshamah</em>-root is exceedingly lofty, but it descended into this world's earthliness for <em>bechirah</em> — so that through this precisely it returns to its source in wondrous completeness. Every person endures many adventures — trials, refinements, ascents and descents without measure — all of Hashem's intention for good: <span class="sq">"everything the Merciful One does is for good."</span> All the refinements and trials are the war of Amalek. One must be expert in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>: <span class="v">"and if I make my bed in She'ol, behold You."</span> When one merits enduring — humiliations, bloodshed, refinements, and trials — this is cutting off Amalek's seed, the lower point, through which one merits teshuvah — <em>Keser</em>, the upper point — Moshe, the Beis HaMikdash. The drawing from upper to lower is the <em>vav</em> within the <em>aleph</em> — appointing a king.
</p>

<p class="p">
This is what Hashem said to Avraham — hinting to every person: <span class="kt"><em>"Lech lecha"</em></span> — go to yourself. The essence of a person, what is called "I," is only the <em>neshamah</em> <span class="vs">(<em>siman</em> 22, Likutay I)</span>. When speaking to a person — "you" or "go" — the intention is the <em>neshamah</em>, the person's essence. The Torah warns: <em>Lech lecha</em> — go to yourself, to your <em>neshamah</em>'s source. All your journeying in this world — intend to go to yourself. <em>"Me'artzecha"</em> — from your earthliness, wherever you descended where it seems hard to leave — overcome and go from your earthliness to your <em>neshamah</em>. <em>"Umimoladtecha"</em> — however you were born, whatever physicality, even though this is a great obstacle — <span class="v">"Behold, in iniquity I was formed"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 51)</span> — go from your birthplace to yourself. <em>"Umibeis avicha"</em> — family, neighbors, relatives among whom you were raised, from whom there are many obstacles — sometimes physically, sometimes through opinions opposed to truth. From all, go forth to your <em>neshamah</em>. Believe that the Holy One Blessed Be He does not come with tyranny against His creatures — you have the power to stand in all this <span class="vs">(Likutay Tinyana, <em>siman</em> 46)</span>.
</p>

<p class="p">
<em>"El ha'aretz asher ar'eka"</em> — Eretz Yisrael, the totality of holiness, the Beis HaMikdash, the source of all <em>neshamos</em>. Rashi: <span class="sq">"He did not reveal the land immediately, to endear it and reward him for each utterance."</span> This is the essential trial: Hashem does not show a person during the trial what he will merit — if one could see, there would be no trial. Who would throw eternal pleasantness away for momentary foolishness? But the trial is that one does not see — great <em>hischazekus</em> is needed. If one overcomes, one will be rewarded for every utterance beseeched before Hashem — then the good will be exceedingly dear, since one never imagined such pleasantness — as several Tzadikim said <span class="vs">(Yerushalmi Avodah Zarah 3:1)</span>: <span class="v">"I said: I have labored in vain… yet surely…"</span>
</p>

<p class="p">
<span class="v">"V'e'escha l'goy gadol va'avarechecha va'agadlah sh'mecha"</span> — the three points. <em>"V'e'escha l'goy gadol"</em> — cutting off Amalek's seed, whose entire war is to cause Hashem's Name to be forgotten and to distance the weak: <span class="v">"and he tailed all the weakened."</span> Whoever subdues him magnifies Hashem's Name by drawing close the distant and weak — "a great nation" through multiplying the holy gathering, the opposite of Amalek's intent. <em>"Va'avarechecha"</em> — drawing blessing from upper to lower point: <span class="v">"like Chermon's dew… for there Hashem commanded blessing"</span> <span class="vs">(Tehillim 133)</span> — appointing a king, through whom <em>shefa</em> is drawn <span class="vs">(Likutay Tinyana <em>siman</em> 16)</span>. <em>"Va'agadlah sh'mecha"</em> — called Avraham with an added <em>hei</em> of <em>da'as</em> — Moshe, the Beis HaMikdash, the upper point. Afterward one still endures ascents and descents — requiring great expertise. Therefore: <span class="v">"<em>Veheyei berachah</em>"</span> — the blessing will endure, one will always remain standing, never falling forever, remaining in the drawing of blessing from upper to lower point.
</p>

<p class="p">
<span class="v">"And Avram went as Hashem spoke, and Lot went with him"</span> — although Avram did as Hashem spoke, <span class="kt">Lot</span> — the <em>yetzer hara</em> — went with him always, refusing to separate, for trial and <em>bechirah</em>. Through this precisely, the highest sparks are refined from the <em>kelipos</em>' depths — as Avraham refined from Lot specifically the soul of Mashiach.
</p>

<p class="p">
<span class="v">"And there was famine in the land, and Avram descended to Mitzrayim"</span> — our Sages say this was a great trial: He tells him to ascend to Eretz Yisrael, then causes him to leave. This is all our words: one must be <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em>, very expert, never falling in mind, never questioning Hashem, only believing <span class="v">"Hashem is righteous and His judgments straight"</span> — all His intention for good. As with Avraham: arriving in Eretz Yisrael, there was famine — forcing him to Mitzrayim, the <em>nafik</em> — yet he withstood the trial until ascending <span class="v">"very laden with cattle, silver, and gold"</span> — sparks refined there. So it passes over every person. As Admo"r z"l writes (<em>siman</em> 8, <em>"Ra'isi Menoras Zahav"</em>): all descents and ascents are hinted in <span class="v">"Avram descended to Mitzrayim"</span> and <span class="v">"Avram ascended from Mitzrayim"</span> — expert in <em>ayeil</em>, expert in <em>nafik</em>.
</p>

<p class="p">
<span class="v">"And he went on his journeys… to the place where his tent had been at first… to the place of the altar he had made there at first, and Avram called there in the Name of Hashem"</span> <span class="vs">(Bereishis 13)</span>. Rashi: <span class="sq">"Also now he called in Hashem's Name."</span> When one overcomes in <em>nafik</em> through all descents until returning to one's place, one must return to the original path — calling in Hashem's Name as at first. He did not fall from his good path through everything that passed — he returns each time to his true path, calling always to Hashem.
</p>

<p class="p">
Also, <span class="v">"there was famine in the land"</span> hints at every person whose going to the marketplace — <em>nafik</em> — is primarily from lacking livelihood. <span class="v">"Avram descended to Mitzrayim"</span> — business preoccupation, the danger of money-lust, the aspect of <em>avodah zarah</em> and Mitzrayim (as in the <em>Ba'al Tefillah</em> story, hinted in <span class="vs">Yeshayah 31</span>: <span class="v">"Woe to those descending to Mitzrayim for help"</span>). But the aspect of Avram — the Jewish person expert in <em>ayeil</em> and <em>nafik</em> — even in Mitzrayim, amidst business, overcomes so as not to become stuck, remembering Hashem, refining holy sparks and ascending with them: <span class="v">"And Avram ascended from Mitzrayim."</span>
</p>

